window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-33"]=[{"id":"q4_cont_078","q":"4:78","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:78 · Death cannot be evaded; good and bad fortune ascribed to the Prophet","source":"Cairo I:530–532","cairo":"I:530–532","source_page_first":530,"source_page_last":532,"ar":"أَيْنَما تَكُونُوا يُدْرِكْكُمُ الْمَوْتُ وَلَوْ كُنْتُمْ فِي بُرُوجٍ مُشَيَّدَةٍ ...\nقلت: (أو أشد) عطف على الكاف النائبة عن المصدر، أي: خشيةً مثل خشية الله أو أشد، وهو من إضافة المصدر إلى المفعول، أي: مثل خشيتهم الله.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: أَلَمْ تَرَ يا محمد إِلَى الَّذِينَ طلبوا منك فرضَ الجهاد حرصًا على أن يجاهدوا، فقيل لهم على لسان الرسول: كُفُّوا أَيْدِيَكُمْ عنه إلى أوَانِ فَرضِه، واشتغلوا بما أُمِرتم به من إقامة الصلاة وإيتاء الزكاة، فَلَمَّا كُتِبَ عَلَيْهِمُ الْقِتالُ دخلهم الخوف إِذا فَرِيقٌ مِنْهُمْ يَخْشَوْنَ النَّاسَ أي:\nالكفار، أن يقتلوهم مثل خشية عقاب اللَّهِ أو أشد خشية منه. وَقالُوا رَبَّنا لِمَ كَتَبْتَ عَلَيْنَا الْقِتالَ في هذا الوقت لَوْلا: هلاّ أَخَّرْتَنا إِلى أَجَلٍ قَرِيبٍ نتمتع فيه بحياتنا أو إلى أن نموت بآجالنا. قلت: والظاهر أنهم قالوا ذلك في نفوسهم، خواطر خطرت لهم، ولم يفوهوا به، إن نزلت فى الصحابة- رضي الله عنهم-، وإن كانت في المنافقين فيمكن أن ينطقوا بها.\nقُلْ مَتاعُ الدُّنْيا قَلِيلٌ وعيشها ذليل، وأجلها قريب، وَالْآخِرَةُ خَيْرٌ لِمَنِ اتَّقى، وحياتها خير وأبقى، وسَتَقدُمون على مولاكم، فيكرم مثواكم، ويوفيكم جزاء أعمالكم، وَلا تُظْلَمُونَ فَتِيلًا\nمن ثواب أعمالكم، ولا تنقصون من أيام أعماركم، جاهدتم أو قعدتم.\n\nأَيْنَما تَكُونُوا يُدْرِكْكُمُ الْمَوْتُ عند انقضاء آجالكم، وَلَوْ كُنْتُمْ فِي بُرُوجٍ مُشَيَّدَةٍ عالية محصنة.\nفإن كان الموت لا بد منه ففي الجهاد أفضل، لأنه حياة لا موتَ بعده. قال الكلبي: نزلت في قوم من الصحابة، منهم: عبد الرحمن بن عوف، والمِقدادُ وقُدامة بن مَظعون وغيرهم، كانوا يُؤذَونَ بمكة، ويستأذنون النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم في القتال، فيقول لهم: كفوا أيديكم حتى يُؤذن فيه لكم، فلما هاجروا إلى المدينة وأُمروا به، كرهه بعضهم كراهية الطبع البشري، فخطر ببالهم شيءٌ مما حكى الله عنهم. فلما كانوا في عين العناية ومحل القرب والهداية عوقبوا على تلك الخواطر، ولو كان غيرهم من أهل البُعد لسُومح له في ذلك، وقيل: نزلت في قوم من المؤمنين أُمروا بالجهاد فنافقوا من الجُبن، وتخلفوا عن الجهاد، وهذا أليق بما بعده من قوله: إِنْ تُصِبْهُمْ حَسَنَةٌ. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: نرى بعض الفقراء يبطشون إلى مقام التجريد ومجاهدة نفوسهم قبل كمال يقينهم، فإذا أُمِروا بذلك، ورأوا ميادين الحروب واشتعال نيران قتل النفوس، وأمروا بالصبر على المكاره، من مواجهة الإنكار ولحوق الذل والافتقار، جبنوا وكلَّوا ورجعوا القهقرى، فيقال لهم: متاعُ الدنيا قليل وعزيزها ذليل، وغنيها فقير، وكبيرها حقير، وما تنالون من الله في جزاء مجاهدتكم خيرٌ وأبقى، ولا تُظلمون فتيلاً من مجاهدتكم لنفوسكم، فلو صبرتم لفزتم بالوصول إلى حضرة ربكم، فلما جبُنتُم ورجعتم، كان جزاؤكم الحرمان، عما ظفر به أهل العرفان.\nوفي مثل هؤلاء يقول ابن الفارض رضي الله عنه:\nتعرّضَ قومٌ للغرامِ وأعرَضوا ... بجانبهم عن صحّتي فيه واعتلّوا\nرضوا بالأماني، وابُتُلوا بحظُوظهم ... وخاضوا بحارَ الحبّ، دعوى، فما ابتلّوا\nفهُم في السُّرى لم يَبرَحوا من مكانهم ... وما ظَعَنوا في السير عنه، وقد كلَّوا\nثم حكى مقالتهم الدالة على نفاقهم، فقال:\n... وَإِنْ تُصِبْهُمْ حَسَنَةٌ يَقُولُوا هذِهِ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ وَإِنْ تُصِبْهُمْ سَيِّئَةٌ يَقُولُوا هذِهِ مِنْ عِنْدِكَ قُلْ كُلٌّ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ فَمالِ هؤُلاءِ الْقَوْمِ لا يَكادُونَ يَفْقَهُونَ حَدِيثاً (78)","quran_ar":"أَيْنَما تَكُونُوا يُدْرِكْكُمُ الْمَوْتُ وَلَوْ كُنْتُمْ فِي بُرُوجٍ مُشَيَّدَةٍ وَإِنْ تُصِبْهُمْ حَسَنَةٌ يَقُولُوا هذِهِ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ وَإِنْ تُصِبْهُمْ سَيِّئَةٌ يَقُولُوا هذِهِ مِنْ عِنْدِكَ قُلْ كُلٌّ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ فَمالِ هؤُلاءِ الْقَوْمِ لا يَكادُونَ يَفْقَهُونَ حَدِيثاً (78)","quran_en":"“Wherever you may be, death will overtake you, even if you are inside strongly built towers. If good befalls them, they say, ‘This is from God’; but if ill befalls them, they say, ‘This is from you.’ Say: All is from God. What ails these people, that they hardly understand any discourse?”","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The outward interpretation, continuing 4:77","html":"<p>Death will meet people at the expiration of their appointed terms, even if they fortify themselves in high and defended towers. Since death is unavoidable, the commentary says, dying in God's path is preferable: it is a life after which there is no death. Al-Kalbī reports that the earlier reproach concerned certain Companions, among them ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, al-Miqdād and Qudāma ibn Maẓʿūn. They suffered mistreatment in Mecca and asked permission to fight, but the Prophet instructed them to restrain their hands until permission came. After the emigration to Medina and the subsequent command, some felt a natural human aversion to fighting, and thoughts of the kind mentioned in the verse passed through their minds. Because they occupied, says Ibn ʿAjība, a place of divine care and closeness, even those thoughts met with rebuke; others further away would have been excused them. Another report says the verses concern believers whose cowardice led them to behave hypocritically and refuse the struggle. Ibn ʿAjība thinks this reading better suits the subsequent reference to those who attribute misfortune to the Prophet. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Death will meet people at the expiration of their appointed terms, even if they fortify themselves in high and defended towers. Since death is unavoidable, the commentary says, dying in God's path is preferable: it is a life after which there is no death. Al-Kalbī reports that the earlier reproach concerned certain Companions, among them ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, al-Miqdād and Qudāma ibn Maẓʿūn. They suffered mistreatment in Mecca and asked permission to fight, but the Prophet instructed them to restrain their hands until permission came. After the emigration to Medina and the subsequent command, some felt a natural human aversion to fighting, and thoughts of the kind mentioned in the verse passed through their minds. Because they occupied, says Ibn ʿAjība, a place of divine care and closeness, even those thoughts met with rebuke; others further away would have been excused them. Another report says the verses concern believers whose cowardice led them to behave hypocritically and refuse the struggle. Ibn ʿAjība thinks this reading better suits the subsequent reference to those who attribute misfortune to the Prophet. God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":", arising from 4:77","html":"<p>Some <em>fuqarāʾ<\/em> strive eagerly for <em>tajrīd<\/em>—stripping away worldly attachments—and combat with their selves before their certainty is fully formed. When they are actually directed to that way and see the battlefields and the fires of slaying egoic desires, and are called to patience under painful things—being criticized, shamed, brought low and reduced to poverty—they grow timid and exhausted and retreat. To them it is said: The world is little; its honoured person is abased, its wealthy person is poor, its great person is insignificant. What God grants as recompense for struggling against oneself is better and more lasting. Not a thread's weight of their striving will be lost. Had they remained patient, they would have arrived in the Presence of their Lord; having retreated in cowardice, their recompense is exclusion from what the people of realized knowing attain.<\/p>\n<p>Ibn ʿAjība quotes Ibn al-Fāriḍ:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Some exposed themselves to love, then turned aside<br>from my health in it, and made excuses.<br>They were content with mere wishes and were tried by their own shares;<br>they crossed the seas of love in claim—yet never became wet.<br>On the night journey they never left their place;<br>they did not depart from it upon the road, for they grew weary.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> Al-Būrīnī, in his commentary on this poem (<em>Sharḥ Dīwān Ibn al-Fāriḍ<\/em>, II:161–162), explains “my health” as steadfastness in love, and <em>iʿtallū<\/em> as offering reasons for turning away. The verb also means “fell ill,” allowing a possible play on health and illness.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Some fuqarāʾ strive eagerly for tajrīd —stripping away worldly attachments—and combat with their selves before their certainty is fully formed. When they are actually directed to that way and see the battlefields and the fires of slaying egoic desires, and are called to patience under painful things—being criticized, shamed, brought low and reduced to poverty—they grow timid and exhausted and retreat. To them it is said: The world is little; its honoured person is abased, its wealthy person is poor, its great person is insignificant. What God grants as recompense for struggling against oneself is better and more lasting. Not a thread's weight of their striving will be lost. Had they remained patient, they would have arrived in the Presence of their Lord; having retreated in cowardice, their recompense is exclusion from what the people of realized knowing attain. Ibn ʿAjība quotes Ibn al-Fāriḍ: Some exposed themselves to love, then turned aside from my health in it, and made excuses. They were content with mere wishes and were tried by their own shares; they crossed the seas of love in claim—yet never became wet. On the night journey they never left their place; they did not depart from it upon the road, for they grew weary. Translator’s note: Al-Būrīnī, in his commentary on this poem ( Sharḥ Dīwān Ibn al-Fāriḍ , II:161–162), explains “my health” as steadfastness in love, and iʿtallū as offering reasons for turning away. The verb also means “fell ill,” allowing a possible play on health and illness."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The outward interpretation, concerning the remainder of 4:78","html":"<p>God now reports a saying that exposes the hypocrites. If they encounter prosperity, abundant crops and manifest blessings, they say that these come from God directly. But if they suffer drought, hunger, deaths or killing, they tell the Messenger that this is due to the ill omen of his arrival and the arrival of his Companions. Ibn ʿAjība attributes a similar accusation to some Jews of Medina: since Muḥammad entered the city, they allegedly said, its fruit diminished and prices rose. The commentator answers indignantly that its fruit rather flourished, prices became favourable, its lights shone and its mysteries appeared. The Prophet had prayed for Medina for blessings like those Abraham prayed for Mecca, and several times as much. Ibn ʿAjība says its blessings have continued to arrive, sensible and spiritual, until the Day of Resurrection.<\/p>\n<p>Other peoples used the same language about their prophets: Ṣāliḥ's people claimed an evil omen from him and his companions [Q 27:47], and Pharaoh's people took bad events as omens from Moses and his followers [Q 7:131]. The Qurʾān declares that earlier messengers were subjected to comparable accusations [cf. Q 41:43]. “Say: All is from God”: the good comes by His generosity and the adverse by His justice. Those who fail to understand this are reproved as being like cattle, or more straying still; they do not comprehend or ponder the Qurʾānic address. Were they to reflect, they would recognise God as Creator and determiner of everything.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God now reports a saying that exposes the hypocrites. If they encounter prosperity, abundant crops and manifest blessings, they say that these come from God directly. But if they suffer drought, hunger, deaths or killing, they tell the Messenger that this is due to the ill omen of his arrival and the arrival of his Companions. Ibn ʿAjība attributes a similar accusation to some Jews of Medina: since Muḥammad entered the city, they allegedly said, its fruit diminished and prices rose. The commentator answers indignantly that its fruit rather flourished, prices became favourable, its lights shone and its mysteries appeared. The Prophet had prayed for Medina for blessings like those Abraham prayed for Mecca, and several times as much. Ibn ʿAjība says its blessings have continued to arrive, sensible and spiritual, until the Day of Resurrection. Other peoples used the same language about their prophets: Ṣāliḥ's people claimed an evil omen from him and his companions [Q 27:47], and Pharaoh's people took bad events as omens from Moses and his followers [Q 7:131]. The Qurʾān declares that earlier messengers were subjected to comparable accusations [cf. Q 41:43]. “Say: All is from God”: the good comes by His generosity and the adverse by His justice. Those who fail to understand this are reproved as being like cattle, or more straying still; they do not comprehend or ponder the Qurʾānic address. Were they to reflect, they would recognise God as Creator and determiner of everything."},{"type":"transition","title":"Transition to 4:79","html":"<p>Ibn ʿAjība then distinguishes the metaphysical statement that all is from God from an <em>adab<\/em> of speech: attribute perfections directly to God, but attribute deficiencies to the baleful effects of one's sins. He begins expounding “Whatever good befalls you is from God” and notes that a servant's obedience could never adequately repay even one divine blessing. He cites the Prophet: “None of you will enter Paradise by his deed.” When asked, “Not even you, Messenger of God?” he replied, “Not even I, unless God enfolds me in His mercy.” The remainder of this explanation continues with verse 79 in the next unit.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Ibn ʿAjība then distinguishes the metaphysical statement that all is from God from an adab of speech: attribute perfections directly to God, but attribute deficiencies to the baleful effects of one's sins. He begins expounding “Whatever good befalls you is from God” and notes that a servant's obedience could never adequately repay even one divine blessing. He cites the Prophet: “None of you will enter Paradise by his deed.” When asked, “Not even you, Messenger of God?” he replied, “Not even I, unless God enfolds me in His mercy.” The remainder of this explanation continues with verse 79 in the next unit."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4780,"arabic_characters":2944},{"id":"q4_cont_079","q":"4:79–80","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:79–80 · Divine causality, the ethics of attribution, and obeying the Messenger","source":"Cairo I:532–534","cairo":"I:532–534","source_page_first":532,"source_page_last":534,"ar":"مَّآ أَصابَكَ مِنْ حَسَنَةٍ فَمِنَ اللَّهِ وَما أَصابَكَ مِنْ سَيِّئَةٍ فَمِنْ نَفْسِكَ ...\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في وصف أهل النفاق: وإنهم إن تُصِبْهُمْ حَسَنَةٌ كخصب ورخاء ونعمة ظاهرة، قالوا: هذِهِ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ، ونسبوها إلى الله بلا واسطة، وَإِنْ تُصِبْهُمْ سَيِّئَةٌ كقحط وجوع وموت وقتل، قالوا للرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: هذِهِ مِنْ عِنْدِكَ بشؤم قدومك أنت وأصحابك، كما قالت اليهود- لعنهم الله-: منذ دخل محمدٌ المدينة نقصت ثمارها وغلت أسعارها.\nقلت: بل زكت ثمارها، ورخصت أسعارها، وأشرقت أنوارها، ولاحت أسرارها، وقد دعا صلّى الله عليه وسلم للمدينة بمثل ما دعا إبراهيمُ لمكة، وأضعاف ذلك، فما زالت الخيرات تترادف إليها حسًا ومعنى إلى يوم القيامة، وهذه المقالة قد صدرت ممن كان قبلهم فقد قالوا لسيدنا صالح عليه السلام: قالُوا اطَّيَّرْنا بِكَ وَبِمَنْ مَعَكَ، وقال تعالى: وَإِنْ تُصِبْهُمْ سَيِّئَةٌ يَطَّيَّرُوا بِمُوسى وَمَنْ مَعَهُ، مَّا يُقالُ لَكَ إِلَّا مَا قَدْ قِيلَ لِلرُّسُلِ مِنْ قَبْلِكَ. قال تعالى مكذبًا لهم: قُلْ كُلٌّ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ الحسنة بفضله، والسيئة بعدله. ثم عيرهم بالجهل فقال: فَمالِ هؤُلاءِ الْقَوْمِ لا يَكادُونَ يَفْقَهُونَ حَدِيثاً فهُم كالبهائم أو أضل سبيلاً، أو لا يفقهون القرآن ويتدبرون حديثه، ولو تدبروا لعلموا أن الكل من عند الله، وأنه خالق كل شيء، المقدَّر لكل شيء.\nثم علَّمنا الأدب بنسبة الكمالات إليه سبحانه بلا واسطة، ونسبة النقائص إلى شؤم ذنوبنا، فقال: مَّآ أَصابَكَ مِنْ حَسَنَةٍ أي: نعمة فَمِنَ اللَّهِ فضلاً وإحسانًا، وأما طاعة العبد فلا تفي بشكر نعمة واحدة، ولذلك قال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «لَن يَدخلَ أحدُكُمُ الجنةَ بعملهِ» ، قيل: ولا أنتَ يا رسول الله؟ قال: «ولا أَنَا، إِلاَّ أنْ يتغمدنى الله برحمته» .\n\nوَما أَصابَكَ أيها الإنسان مِنْ سَيِّئَةٍ أي: بلية فَمِنْ نَفْسِكَ أي: شؤم ذنبك، وعنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- أنه قال: «ما من خَدشٍ بعُود ولا اختلاج عرق ولا غيره إلا بذنب، وما يعفو الله عنه أكثرُ» . فلا ينافي قوله: قُلْ كُلٌّ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ فإن الكل منه إيجاداً واختراعًا، غير أن الحسنة إحسانٌ، والسيئة مُجَازاة وانتقام. كما قالت عائشة- رضى الله عنها-: «مَا مِن مُسلمٍ يُصيبه وَصَبٌ ولا نَصَبٌ، حتى الشوكة يُشَاكها، وحتى انقطاع شِسع نَعلهِ، إلا بذنب، وما يعفو الله عنه أكثر» .\nوفي مصحف ابن مسعود: (قالوا مَّآ أَصَابَكَ مِنْ حَسَنَةٍ فمن الله) الآية، فتكون حينئٍذ من مقالة المنافقين، والآيتان كما ترى لا حجة فيها للمعتزلة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: ثلاث خصال لا ينجو منها إلا القليل كما في الحديث: الطيرة، والحسد، والظن. فقال- عليه الصلاة السلام: «إذا تَطَيَّرتَ فامضِ، وإذا حسدتَ فلا تبغ، وإذا ظننت فلا تُحَقّق» . فيتأكد على المريد أن يتطهر من هذه الخصال، ويصفي مشربه من التوحيد، فلا يرى في الوجود إلا مولاه، ولا ينسب التأثير إلى شيء سواه، إذا رأى نعمة به أو بغيره، قال: من الله، وإذا رأى مصيبة كذلك تأدب مع الله، فيعتقد في قلبه أنها من قَدَرِ الله، يقول:\nقُلْ كُلٌّ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ، وينسب النقص إلى نفسه وهواه، فالنفس والشيطان مناديل الحضرة، تمسح فيهما أوساخ الأقدار، وَرَبُّكَ يَخْلُقُ مَا يَشاءُ وَيَخْتارُ. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم شَهِدَ جلّ جلاله لرسوله بالرسالة، تحريضًا على تعظيمه وحثًا على طاعته، وترهيبًا من سوء الأدب معه، كما صدر من المنافقين، فقال:\n... وَأَرْسَلْناكَ لِلنَّاسِ رَسُولاً وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ شَهِيداً (79) مَنْ يُطِعِ الرَّسُولَ فَقَدْ أَطاعَ اللَّهَ وَمَنْ تَوَلَّى فَما أَرْسَلْناكَ عَلَيْهِمْ حَفِيظاً (80)\nقلت: إن تعلق الجار بالفعل كان (رسولاً) حال مؤكدة، وإن تعلق بالاسم كان حالاً مؤسسة تفيد العموم أي أرسلناك رسولاً للناس جميعًا، و (حفيظًا) حال من الكاف.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَأَرْسَلْناكَ يا محمد لِلنَّاسِ رَسُولًا تعلمهم التوحيد وتدلهم على الأدب، فالتوحيد محله البواطن، فلا يرى الفعل إلا من الله، والأدب محله الظواهر فينسب بلسانه النقص إلى نفسه وهواه. وإذا شهد الحق- جلّ جلاله- لرسوله بالرسالة أغْنَى عن غيره، وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ شَهِيداً. وشهادة الحق له بالمعجزات الواضحات، والبراهين القطعيات، والدلائل السمعيات، فإذا ثبتت رسالته وجب على الناس طاعته، ولذلك قال: مَنْ يُطِعِ الرَّسُولَ فَقَدْ أَطاعَ اللَّهَ لأنه مُبلغ عن الله لا ينطق عن الهوى. رُوِي أنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم قال:\n«مَن أطَاعَنِي فَقَد أطَاعَ الله، ومَن أحبَني فقَد أحبَ الله» فقال بعض المنافقين: ما يريد هذا الرجل إلا أن نتخذه ربًّا، كما اتخذت النصارى عيسى. فنزل: مَنْ يُطِعِ الرَّسُولَ فَقَدْ أَطاعَ اللَّهَ وَمَنْ تَوَلَّى وأعرض فَما أَرْسَلْناكَ عَلَيْهِمْ حَفِيظاً تَحفَظُ عليهم أعمالهم، وتحاسبهم عليها، إنما عليك البلاغ وعلينا الحساب.\nالإشارة: كما شهد الحق- جل جلاله- لرسله بالرسالة، بما أظهر لهم من المعجزات، شهد لأوليائه بالولاية بما منحهم من الكرامات. والمراد بالكرامة: هي تحقيق العرفان، ومعرفة الذوق والوجدان، واستقامة الظواهر والبواطن، وتهذيب الأخلاق وهداية الناس على يديه إلى العليم الخلاق، فهذه الكرامة المعتبرة عند المحققين، فمن أطاعهم فقد أطاع الله، ومن أعرض عنهم فقد أعرض عن معرفة الله، ومن أحبهم فقد أحب الله، ومن أبغضهم فقد أبغض الله لأنهم نور من أنوار الله، وعين من عيون الله، إذ لم يبق فيهم بقية مما سوى الله، أقدامهم على قدم رسول الله، «إِنَّ الذين يُبَايِعُونَكَ إنما يبايعون الله» . فافهم، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر أحوال أهل النفاق، فقال:","quran_ar":"مَّآ أَصابَكَ مِنْ حَسَنَةٍ فَمِنَ اللَّهِ وَما أَصابَكَ مِنْ سَيِّئَةٍ فَمِنْ نَفْسِكَ وَأَرْسَلْناكَ لِلنَّاسِ رَسُولاً وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ شَهِيداً (79) مَنْ يُطِعِ الرَّسُولَ فَقَدْ أَطاعَ اللَّهَ وَمَنْ تَوَلَّى فَما أَرْسَلْناكَ عَلَيْهِمْ حَفِيظاً (80)","quran_en":"“Whatever good befalls you is from God, and whatever ill befalls you is from yourself. We have sent you to humankind as a messenger; God suffices as witness. Whoever obeys the Messenger has obeyed God; and whoever turns away—We have not sent you to be a keeper over them.”","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The outward interpretation, continuing from 4:78","html":"<p>Whatever adversity (<em>sayyiʾa<\/em>) comes upon a person, says Ibn ʿAjība, is due to his own self: the baleful consequence of sin. He cites a report attributed to the Prophet saying that even a scratch from a stick, a twitching vein, or another minor affliction occurs because of a sin, while God forgives much more. This does not contradict “Say: All is from God” [4:78]. Every event comes from God in the sense of being brought into existence and originated (<em>ījād<\/em> and <em>ikhtirāʿ<\/em>); yet good is His beneficence, whereas the adverse is requital and chastisement. He also attributes to ʿĀʾisha a report that no Muslim suffers fatigue, illness, the prick of a thorn, or even the snapping of a sandal-strap except through some sin, while God forgives far more. [Translator’s note: The specific extensions in these two attributions require comparison with their transmitted witnesses.]<\/p>\n<p>Ibn ʿAjība notes a wording attributed to the codex of Ibn Masʿūd, adding “They said” at the start of “Whatever good befalls you is from God.” On that reported reading, the sentence is understood as continuing the speech of the hypocrites. [Translator’s note: This is cited as an early exegetical reading, not presented as part of the standard transmitted Qurʾānic text.] Neither this verse nor the preceding, he insists, furnishes an argument for the Muʿtazilites as against his theological account of divine creation. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Whatever adversity ( sayyiʾa ) comes upon a person, says Ibn ʿAjība, is due to his own self: the baleful consequence of sin. He cites a report attributed to the Prophet saying that even a scratch from a stick, a twitching vein, or another minor affliction occurs because of a sin, while God forgives much more. This does not contradict “Say: All is from God” [4:78]. Every event comes from God in the sense of being brought into existence and originated ( ījād and ikhtirāʿ ); yet good is His beneficence, whereas the adverse is requital and chastisement. He also attributes to ʿĀʾisha a report that no Muslim suffers fatigue, illness, the prick of a thorn, or even the snapping of a sandal-strap except through some sin, while God forgives far more. [Translator’s note: The specific extensions in these two attributions require comparison with their transmitted witnesses.] Ibn ʿAjība notes a wording attributed to the codex of Ibn Masʿūd, adding “They said” at the start of “Whatever good befalls you is from God.” On that reported reading, the sentence is understood as continuing the speech of the hypocrites. [Translator’s note: This is cited as an early exegetical reading, not presented as part of the standard transmitted Qurʾānic text.] Neither this verse nor the preceding, he insists, furnishes an argument for the Muʿtazilites as against his theological account of divine creation. God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":", concerning attribution and purification","html":"<p>Ibn ʿAjība mentions the report that only a few escape three traits: taking evil omens (<em>ṭiyara<\/em>), envy (<em>ḥasad<\/em>) and suspicion (<em>ẓann<\/em>). In its cited form the Prophet advises: “If you take an omen, proceed; if you envy, do not transgress; if you suspect, do not investigate [as if it were certainty].” The aspirant must cleanse himself of these habits and purify the drink of his tawḥīd. He sees only his Lord in being, attributing no efficacy to anything besides Him. Seeing a blessing in himself or another, he says, “It is from God.” Seeing calamity, he still holds inwardly that it comes by God's decree, as the Qurʾān says, “All is from God,” while in speech he courteously attributes deficiency to his own self and passions. The self and Satan, in Ibn ʿAjība's bold image, are the “cloths of the Presence” upon which the stains of decreed contingencies are wiped. “Your Lord creates what He wills and chooses” [Q 28:68]. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Ibn ʿAjība mentions the report that only a few escape three traits: taking evil omens ( ṭiyara ), envy ( ḥasad ) and suspicion ( ẓann ). In its cited form the Prophet advises: “If you take an omen, proceed; if you envy, do not transgress; if you suspect, do not investigate [as if it were certainty].” The aspirant must cleanse himself of these habits and purify the drink of his tawḥīd. He sees only his Lord in being, attributing no efficacy to anything besides Him. Seeing a blessing in himself or another, he says, “It is from God.” Seeing calamity, he still holds inwardly that it comes by God's decree, as the Qurʾān says, “All is from God,” while in speech he courteously attributes deficiency to his own self and passions. The self and Satan, in Ibn ʿAjība's bold image, are the “cloths of the Presence” upon which the stains of decreed contingencies are wiped. “Your Lord creates what He wills and chooses” [Q 28:68]. God knows best."},{"type":"language","title":"Grammar, now on the Messenger (*qultu*)","html":"<p>The Exalted One then bears witness to His Messenger’s mission, urging reverence for him and obedience to him, and warning against discourtesy toward him such as the hypocrites displayed.<\/p>\n<p>If the prepositional phrase “to humankind” depends on the verb “We sent,” the word “messenger” (<em>rasūlan<\/em>) is a confirming circumstantial accusative (<em>ḥāl muʾakkida<\/em>). If it depends instead on “messenger,” it is an establishing circumstance (<em>ḥāl muʾassisa<\/em>) which expresses universality: We sent you <strong>as messenger to all humankind<\/strong>. “Keeper” (<em>ḥafīẓan<\/em>) is a circumstance of the second-person object-pronoun.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The Exalted One then bears witness to His Messenger’s mission, urging reverence for him and obedience to him, and warning against discourtesy toward him such as the hypocrites displayed. If the prepositional phrase “to humankind” depends on the verb “We sent,” the word “messenger” ( rasūlan ) is a confirming circumstantial accusative ( ḥāl muʾakkida ). If it depends instead on “messenger,” it is an establishing circumstance ( ḥāl muʾassisa ) which expresses universality: We sent you as messenger to all humankind . “Keeper” ( ḥafīẓan ) is a circumstance of the second-person object-pronoun."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The outward interpretation, concerning 4:79b–80","html":"<p>God has sent Muḥammad to humankind to teach divine unity and guide people into proper comportment (<em>adab<\/em>). The inward place of tawḥīd is the heart: it recognises action as coming only from God. The outward place of adab is conduct and speech: the servant assigns imperfection to his own self and its cravings. God's testimony to the Messenger's mission suffices without anyone else's endorsement—“God suffices as witness”—a testimony given by clear miracles, decisive demonstrations and transmitted proofs. Once the mission is established, people are bound to obey him. Whoever obeys the Messenger obeys God, for he transmits God's command and does not speak by mere caprice. Ibn ʿAjība reports the saying: “Whoever obeys me has obeyed God; whoever loves me has loved God,” and a polemical anecdote in which hypocrites objected that the Messenger sought to be made a lord like Jesus among Christians. The revelation replied that obedience to him is obedience to God. But the one who turns away is not entrusted to the Messenger as a keeper of every deed and its reckoning: his commission is to convey, whereas the account belongs to God.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God has sent Muḥammad to humankind to teach divine unity and guide people into proper comportment ( adab ). The inward place of tawḥīd is the heart: it recognises action as coming only from God. The outward place of adab is conduct and speech: the servant assigns imperfection to his own self and its cravings. God's testimony to the Messenger's mission suffices without anyone else's endorsement—“God suffices as witness”—a testimony given by clear miracles, decisive demonstrations and transmitted proofs. Once the mission is established, people are bound to obey him. Whoever obeys the Messenger obeys God, for he transmits God's command and does not speak by mere caprice. Ibn ʿAjība reports the saying: “Whoever obeys me has obeyed God; whoever loves me has loved God,” and a polemical anecdote in which hypocrites objected that the Messenger sought to be made a lord like Jesus among Christians. The revelation replied that obedience to him is obedience to God. But the one who turns away is not entrusted to the Messenger as a keeper of every deed and its reckoning: his commission is to convey, whereas the account belongs to God."},{"type":"spiritual","title":", concerning 4:80","html":"<p>As God testified to the mission of His messengers through miracles (<em>muʿjizāt<\/em>), so, says Ibn ʿAjība, He testifies to the sanctity (<em>wilāya<\/em>) of His friends through extraordinary graces (<em>karāmāt<\/em>). The <em>karāma<\/em> that matters to those of realization is the attainment of knowing God (<em>ʿirfān<\/em>) through experience and taste (<em>dhawq<\/em> and <em>wijdān<\/em>), the uprightness of outward and inward conduct, the refinement of character, and the guidance of people through a saint toward the All-Knowing Creator. These, rather than wonders for their own sake, constitute the grace esteemed by the accomplished. Whoever obeys them has obeyed God; to turn away from them is to turn away from knowing God. Whoever loves them has loved God, and whoever hates them has hated God, because they are a light among God’s lights and an eye among God’s eyes: no trace of anything other than God remains in them. Their footsteps follow the footsteps of the Messenger. Ibn ʿAjība adduces [Q 48:10], “Those who pledge allegiance to you pledge allegiance only to God,” and closes: Understand. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"As God testified to the mission of His messengers through miracles ( muʿjizāt ), so, says Ibn ʿAjība, He testifies to the sanctity ( wilāya ) of His friends through extraordinary graces ( karāmāt ). The karāma that matters to those of realization is the attainment of knowing God ( ʿirfān ) through experience and taste ( dhawq and wijdān ), the uprightness of outward and inward conduct, the refinement of character, and the guidance of people through a saint toward the All-Knowing Creator. These, rather than wonders for their own sake, constitute the grace esteemed by the accomplished. Whoever obeys them has obeyed God; to turn away from them is to turn away from knowing God. Whoever loves them has loved God, and whoever hates them has hated God, because they are a light among God’s lights and an eye among God’s eyes: no trace of anything other than God remains in them. Their footsteps follow the footsteps of the Messenger. Ibn ʿAjība adduces [Q 48:10], “Those who pledge allegiance to you pledge allegiance only to God,” and closes: Understand. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The discourse returns to the conduct of hypocrites.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The discourse returns to the conduct of hypocrites."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":5208,"arabic_characters":4719},{"id":"q4_cont_081","q":"4:81","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:81 · Profession of obedience and secret contradiction","source":"Cairo I:534","cairo":"I:534","source_page_first":534,"source_page_last":534,"ar":"وَيَقُولُونَ طاعَةٌ فَإِذا بَرَزُوا مِنْ عِنْدِكَ بَيَّتَ طائِفَةٌ مِنْهُمْ غَيْرَ الَّذِي تَقُولُ وَاللَّهُ يَكْتُبُ ما يُبَيِّتُونَ فَأَعْرِضْ عَنْهُمْ وَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى اللَّهِ وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ وَكِيلاً (81)\nقلت: (طاعة) : خبر، أي: أمرنا طاعة، وأصله النصب على المصدر، ورُفِعَ للدلالة على الثبوت، وبيَّتَ الشيء: دبَّره ليلاً وأضمره في نفسه.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في شأن المنافقين: وَيَقُولُونَ لك إذا حضروا معك: أمرنا وشأننا طاعَةٌ لك فيما تأمرنا به، فَإِذا بَرَزُوا أي: خرجوا مِنْ عِنْدِكَ بَيَّتَ طائِفَةٌ مِنْهُمْ أي: دبَّرت ليلاً وأخفت من النفاق غَيْرَ الَّذِي تَقُولُ لك من قبول الإيمان وإظهار الطاعة، أو زوَّرت خلاف ما قلتَ لها من الأمر بالطاعة، وَاللَّهُ يَكْتُبُ ما يُبَيِّتُونَ أي: يُثبِتُه في صحائفهم فيجازيهم عليه، فَأَعْرِضْ عَنْهُمْ ولا تبال بهم، وَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى اللَّهِ يكفك شرهم، وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ وَكِيلًا عليهم، فسينتقم لك منهم.\nالإشارة: هذه الخصلة موجودة في بعض العوام إذا حضروا مع أهل الخصوصية أظهروا الطاعة والإقرار، وإذا خرجوا عنهم بيَّتوا الانتقاد والإنكار، فلا يليق إلا الإعراض عنهم، والغيبة في الله عنهم، فإن الله يكفي شرهم بكفالته وحفظه. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم دلّهم على ما فيه دواء مرض قلوبهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَيَقُولُونَ طاعَةٌ فَإِذا بَرَزُوا مِنْ عِنْدِكَ بَيَّتَ طائِفَةٌ مِنْهُمْ غَيْرَ الَّذِي تَقُولُ وَاللَّهُ يَكْتُبُ ما يُبَيِّتُونَ فَأَعْرِضْ عَنْهُمْ وَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى اللَّهِ وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ وَكِيلاً (81)","quran_en":"“They say, ‘Obedience!’ But when they have gone forth from your presence, a group of them plots by night something other than what you say. God records what they plot at night. So turn away from them, and put your trust in God; God suffices as guardian.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p><em>Ṭāʿa<\/em> (“obedience”) stands in the nominative as a predicate, its implied subject being “our affair”: “Our business is obedience.” Its underlying form could have been an accusative verbal noun, <em>ṭāʿatan<\/em>, but the nominative suggests permanence and established character. To <em>bayyata<\/em> something is to devise it at night and conceal it inwardly.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Ṭāʿa (“obedience”) stands in the nominative as a predicate, its implied subject being “our affair”: “Our business is obedience.” Its underlying form could have been an accusative verbal noun, ṭāʿatan , but the nominative suggests permanence and established character. To bayyata something is to devise it at night and conceal it inwardly."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>The hypocrites say, when they sit with the Messenger, that their affair is complete obedience to whatever he commands. But once they leave him, a faction arranges matters secretly at night and conceals hypocrisy contrary to its outward declaration of faith and acceptance of obedience. Or they distort and fabricate something different from the command he had given them. God writes down their nocturnal designs in their scrolls and will recompense them for them. The Messenger should turn aside from them, pay them no heed and trust in God, who will suffice against their malice. God is enough as trustee (<em>wakīl<\/em>), and will exact a reckoning on his behalf.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The hypocrites say, when they sit with the Messenger, that their affair is complete obedience to whatever he commands. But once they leave him, a faction arranges matters secretly at night and conceals hypocrisy contrary to its outward declaration of faith and acceptance of obedience. Or they distort and fabricate something different from the command he had given them. God writes down their nocturnal designs in their scrolls and will recompense them for them. The Messenger should turn aside from them, pay them no heed and trust in God, who will suffice against their malice. God is enough as trustee ( wakīl ), and will exact a reckoning on his behalf."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Ibn ʿAjība finds an analogous trait among some ordinary people. In the company of people distinguished by spiritual realization they display agreement and obedience; on leaving they secretly prepare criticism and repudiation. The appropriate response is not to struggle with them, but to turn aside and become absent in God from concern over them. God's care and protection will suffice against their harm. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Ibn ʿAjība finds an analogous trait among some ordinary people. In the company of people distinguished by spiritual realization they display agreement and obedience; on leaving they secretly prepare criticism and repudiation. The appropriate response is not to struggle with them, but to turn aside and become absent in God from concern over them. God's care and protection will suffice against their harm. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Qurʾān now directs such people to a remedy for the disease of their hearts.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The Qurʾān now directs such people to a remedy for the disease of their hearts."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1497,"arabic_characters":1123},{"id":"q4_cont_082","q":"4:82","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:82 · Contemplating the Qurʾān and the unveiling of meaning","source":"Cairo I:534–535","cairo":"I:534–535","source_page_first":534,"source_page_last":535,"ar":"أَفَلا يَتَدَبَّرُونَ الْقُرْآنَ وَلَوْ كانَ مِنْ عِنْدِ غَيْرِ اللَّهِ لَوَجَدُوا فِيهِ اخْتِلافاً كَثِيراً (82)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: أفلا يتدبر هؤلاء المنافقون الْقُرْآنَ، وينظرون ما فيه من البلاغة والبيان، ويتبصّرون في معاني علومه وأسراره، ويطلعون على عجائب قصصه وأخباره، وتَوافُق آياتهِ وأحكامه، حتى يتحققوا أنه ليس من طوق البشر، وإنما هو من عند الله الواحد القهار، وَلَوْ كانَ مِنْ عِنْدِ غَيْرِ اللَّهِ لَوَجَدُوا فِيهِ اخْتِلافاً كَثِيراً بَين أحكامه وآياته، من تَفَاوتِ اللفظ وتناقض المعنى، وكَون بعضه فصيحًا، وبعضه ركيكًا، وبعضه تصعب معارضته وبعضه تسهل، وبعضه توافق أخباره المستقبلة للواقع، وبعضه لا يوافق، وبعضه يوافق العقل، وبعضه لا يوافقه، على ما دلّ عليه الاستقراء من أن كلام البشر، إذا طال، قطعًا يوجد فيه شيء من الخلل والتناقض.\nقال البيضاوي: ولعل ذكره للتنبيه على أن اختلاف ما سبق من الأحكام ليس للتناقض في الحكم، بل لاختلاف الأحوال من الحكم والمصالح. هـ. قال ابن جزي: وإن عَرَضَت لأحدٍ شبهةٌ وظن اختلافًا في شيء من القرآن، فالواجب أن يَتَّهِمَ نظره، ويسأل أهل العلم ويطالع تآليفهم، حتى يعلم أن ذلك ليس باختلاف. هـ.\nالإشارة: تدبر القرآن على حسب صفاء الجنان، فبقدر ما يتطهر القلب من حب الدنيا والهوى تتجلى فيه أسرار كلام المولى، وبقدر ما يتراكم في مرآة قلبه من صور الأكوان، ينحجب عن أسرار معاني القرآن ولو كان من أكابر علماء اللسان. فلما كان القرآن هو دواء لمرض القلوب، أمر الله المنافقين بالتدبر فى معانيه لعل ذلك المرض ينقلع عن قلوبهم، لكن الأقفال التي على القلوب مَنَعَت القلوبَ من فهم كلام علام الغيوب، فحلاوة كلام الله لا يذوقها إلا أهل التجريد، الخائضون في تيار بحار التوحيد، الذين صَفَت قلوبُهم من الأغيار، وتطهرت من الأكدار، يتمتعون أولاً بحلاوة الكلام، ثم يتمتعون ثانيا بحلاوة شهود المتكلم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nومن مساوئ المنافقين إفشاء أسرار المؤمنين، كما قال تعالى:","quran_ar":"أَفَلا يَتَدَبَّرُونَ الْقُرْآنَ وَلَوْ كانَ مِنْ عِنْدِ غَيْرِ اللَّهِ لَوَجَدُوا فِيهِ اخْتِلافاً كَثِيراً (82)","quran_en":"“Will they not contemplate the Qurʾān? Had it been from anyone other than God, they would have found within it much inconsistency.”","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Why do these hypocrites not reflect on (<em>yatadabbarūn<\/em>) the Qurʾān—its eloquence and lucidity, the sciences and mysteries contained in its meanings, the wonders of its narratives and accounts, and the concord of its verses and judgments? Attentive study would disclose that such a discourse lies beyond human capacity and is from God, the One, the Overpowering. Had it originated from another than God, its laws and verses would reveal frequent conflict: inequalities of diction, contradictions of meaning, some parts eloquent and others poor, some passages difficult to imitate and others easy, some predictions agreeing with subsequent events and others failing, some assertions agreeing with reason and others not. Human discourse, when extended long enough, is bound, as induction shows, to exhibit faults or inconsistencies.<\/p>\n<p>Al-Bayḍāwī adds that the verse may be intended to show that differences among the judgments previously mentioned do not indicate contradictory legislation; they arise from differences in circumstances, with their respective purposes of wisdom (<em>ḥikam<\/em>) and benefits (<em>maṣāliḥ<\/em>). Ibn Juzayy says that if someone encounters a troubling question and supposes some disagreement exists within the Qurʾān, he should first suspect his own reading, consult people of knowledge and examine their works until he understands that the appearance is not an actual contradiction. Here end the cited observations.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Why do these hypocrites not reflect on ( yatadabbarūn ) the Qurʾān—its eloquence and lucidity, the sciences and mysteries contained in its meanings, the wonders of its narratives and accounts, and the concord of its verses and judgments? Attentive study would disclose that such a discourse lies beyond human capacity and is from God, the One, the Overpowering. Had it originated from another than God, its laws and verses would reveal frequent conflict: inequalities of diction, contradictions of meaning, some parts eloquent and others poor, some passages difficult to imitate and others easy, some predictions agreeing with subsequent events and others failing, some assertions agreeing with reason and others not. Human discourse, when extended long enough, is bound, as induction shows, to exhibit faults or inconsistencies. Al-Bayḍāwī adds that the verse may be intended to show that differences among the judgments previously mentioned do not indicate contradictory legislation; they arise from differences in circumstances, with their respective purposes of wisdom ( ḥikam ) and benefits ( maṣāliḥ ). Ibn Juzayy says that if someone encounters a troubling question and supposes some disagreement exists within the Qurʾān, he should first suspect his own reading, consult people of knowledge and examine their works until he understands that the appearance is not an actual contradiction. Here end the cited observations."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>The capacity to ponder the Qurʾān corresponds to the cleanness of the heart (<em>ṣafāʾ al-janān<\/em>). In the measure that the heart is purified of love of the world and passionate inclination (<em>hawā<\/em>), the mysteries of the Lord's speech are disclosed within it. In the measure that images of created things accumulate upon the heart's mirror, the deeper significations of the Qurʾān are hidden from it—even if its possessor is among the greatest experts in the Arabic language. Because the Qurʾān is a medicine for diseased hearts, the hypocrites are commanded to contemplate its meanings in the hope that their disease may be uprooted. Yet the locks upon their hearts have kept them from understanding the speech of the Knower of all unseen things.<\/p>\n<p>Only people of <em>tajrīd<\/em>, whose hearts have been emptied of other-than-God (<em>aghyār<\/em>) and cleansed of clouding impurities (<em>akdār<\/em>), who have plunged into the streaming seas of divine unity (<em>tawḥīd<\/em>), taste the sweetness of God's words. First they enjoy the sweetness of the speech (<em>kalām<\/em>); then they enjoy the sweetness of witnessing the One who speaks (<em>al-Mutakallim<\/em>). God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"The capacity to ponder the Qurʾān corresponds to the cleanness of the heart ( ṣafāʾ al-janān ). In the measure that the heart is purified of love of the world and passionate inclination ( hawā ), the mysteries of the Lord's speech are disclosed within it. In the measure that images of created things accumulate upon the heart's mirror, the deeper significations of the Qurʾān are hidden from it—even if its possessor is among the greatest experts in the Arabic language. Because the Qurʾān is a medicine for diseased hearts, the hypocrites are commanded to contemplate its meanings in the hope that their disease may be uprooted. Yet the locks upon their hearts have kept them from understanding the speech of the Knower of all unseen things. Only people of tajrīd , whose hearts have been emptied of other-than-God ( aghyār ) and cleansed of clouding impurities ( akdār ), who have plunged into the streaming seas of divine unity ( tawḥīd ), taste the sweetness of God's words. First they enjoy the sweetness of the speech ( kalām ); then they enjoy the sweetness of witnessing the One who speaks ( al-Mutakallim ). God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Among the blameworthy acts of the hypocrites is the disclosure of the believers' secrets.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Among the blameworthy acts of the hypocrites is the disclosure of the believers' secrets."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2650,"arabic_characters":1688},{"id":"q4_cont_083","q":"4:83","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:83 · News, *istinbāṭ*, the guarding of secrets, and the complementarity of knowledge","source":"Cairo I:535–538","cairo":"I:535–538","source_page_first":535,"source_page_last":538,"ar":"وَإِذا جاءَهُمْ أَمْرٌ مِنَ الْأَمْنِ أَوِ الْخَوْفِ أَذاعُوا بِهِ وَلَوْ رَدُّوهُ إِلَى الرَّسُولِ وَإِلى أُولِي الْأَمْرِ مِنْهُمْ لَعَلِمَهُ الَّذِينَ يَسْتَنْبِطُونَهُ مِنْهُمْ ...\nقلت: استنبط الشيء: استخرجه من غيره، وأصل الاستنباط: إخراج النبط، وهو الماء، يخرج من البئر أول ما يحفر، والجار في (منهم) : إما بيان للموصول، أي: لعلم المستنبطون الذين هم أولوا الأمر، أو يتعلق ب (علم) ، أي:\nلعلمه الذين يستخرجونه إلى الناس من أولي الأمر.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: في ذمّ المنافقين أو ضعفة المسلمين: وَإِذا جاءَهُمْ أَمْرٌ أي: خبر عن السرايا الذين توجهوا للغزو، من نصر وغنيمة وأمن أو خوف، وقتل وهزيمة، أَذاعُوا بِهِ أي: تحدثوا به، وأشهروه، وأرجفوا به قبل أن يصل إلى الرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام- وأكابر الصحابة، الذين هم أولو الأمر وأهل البصائر، فيعرفون كيف يتحدثون به.\nولو ردوا ذلك إِلَى الرَّسُولِ وأخبروه به سرًا، أو سكتوا حتى يصل إليه، أو يردوه إِلى أُولِي الْأَمْرِ من أكابر الصحابة، لعلمه الذين يستخرجونه إلى الناس مِنْهُمْ، فينقلونه على وجهه، ويعرفون كيف يتحدثون به من غير إرجاف ولا تخويف، أو لَعَلِمَهُ الَّذِينَ يَسْتَنْبِطُونَهُ وهم أولو الأمر أولاً، ثم يعلم الناس، فلا يكون فيه إرجاف ولا سوء أدب. أو: وإذا جاءهم أمر من وحي السماء: من تخويف أو تأمين، أذاعوا به قبل أن يظهره الرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام-، ولو سكتوا وردوا ذلك إلى الرسول حتى يتحدث به للناس، ويظهره أولو الأمر من أكابر أصحابه، لعلمه الذين يستخرجون ذلك الوحي من أصله، وهو الرسول- عليه الصلاة السلام- وأكابر أصحابه، كما فعل عمر رضي الله عنه: إذ سمع أن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم طلق نساءه، فدخل عليه فقال: أطلقت نساءك؟ قال:\n«لا» ، فقام على باب المسجد، فقال: إن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم لم يطلق نساءه، فأنزل الله هذه القصة، قال: وأنا الذي استنبطته. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: قالت الحكماء: قلوب الأحرار قبور الأسرار، وهذه الخصلة التي ذمَّها الله تعالى تُوجد في كثير من العوام مهما سمعوا خبرًا: خيرًا أو شرًا، بادروا إلى إفشائه، ولا سيما إذا سمعوه على أهل النسبة أو أهل الخصوصية، وقد تُوجد في بعض الفقراء، وهي غفلة ونوع من الفضول، فالفقير الصادق غائب عن أخبار الزمان وأهله، وقد ترك الناسَ وما هم فيه، وقد تغلب عليه الغيبة في الله حتى تغيب عنه الأيام، وأما الفقير الذي يتسمع الأخبار ويبحث عنها فلا نسبة له في الفقر، إلا اسم بلا مسمى، وقد ترى بعض الفقراء، يبلّغ مساوئ إخوانه إلى المشايخ، وهو سبب الطرد، والعياذ بالله. وقد كان صلّى الله عليه وسلم يقول: «لا تبلغونى مساوئ أصحابي (١)» لأن ذلك يسؤوهم، والخير كله في إدخال السرور على قلوب المشايخ.\nوتنسحب الآية على مَن يُفشِيِ أسرار الربوبية، ويُطلع الفقراء علَى الحقيقة، ولو ردوا ذلك إلى شيخهم حتى يكون هو الذي يطلعهم لكان أحسن، لأن الحقيقة إذا أُخِذَت من الشيخ كان فيها سر كبير، بخلاف ما إذا أُخِذّت من غيره، إلاَّ إذا كان مأذونا له في ذلك فكأنه هو. والله تعالى أعلم.\nوقال الورتجبي: قال أبو سعيد الخراز: إن له عبادًا يدخل عليهم الخلل، ولولا ذلك لفسدوا وتعطلوا، وذلك أنهم بَلَغُوا من العلم غاية، صاروا إلى علم المجهول، الذي لم ينصُّه كتاب، ولا جاء به خبر، لكن العقلاء العارفون، يحتجون له من الكتاب والسنة، بحسن استنباطهم ومعرفتهم، قال الله تعالى: لَعَلِمَهُ الَّذِينَ يَسْتَنْبِطُونَهُ مِنْهُمْ. هـ.\nقلت: ومعنى كلامه: أن الله- تعالى- أشغل علماء الظاهر بتقرير علم الفرق، ولولا اشتغالهم بذلك لتعطلوا وتبطلوا، إذ لا قدرة لهم على عمل القلوب من الفكرة والنظرة، لكن العارفون يقرون لهم ذلك، ويحتجون لهم بما في نشر العلم من الأجور، من الكتاب والسنة، لأنهم قاموا بنظام علم الحكمة ورفعوا علم الشريعة، ولولا قيامهم بذلك لتعين على أهل الباطن، فتتشوش عليهم قلوبهم، وكان شيخ شيوخنا سيدي علي الجمل العمراني رضي الله عنه يقول:\nجزاهم الله عنا خيرًا رفعوا لنا علم الشريعة، نحن نغرق في البحر، ثم نرفع رأسنا فنرى العلم قائمًا، ثم نرجع إلى البحر. هـ. بالمعنى، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم إن الهداية بيد الله، قوم أقامهم في الفرق، وقوم هداهم إلى الجمع، كما قال تعالى:\n... وَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَتُهُ لاتَّبَعْتُمُ الشَّيْطانَ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً (83)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: لولا أن الله تفضل عليكم ورحمكم بنبي الرحمة، وأنقذكم من متابعة الشيطان وعبادة الأوثان، لبقيتم على كفركم وضلالكم، ولا تبعتم الشيطان فيما يأمركم به من الكفر والعصيان، إلا قليلاً ممن اهتدى قبل بعثته، كقس بن ساعدة، وزيد بن نفيل، وورقة بن نوفل، رزقهم الله كمال العقل فنظروا وتفكروا بعقولهم فوجدوا الله واعتزلوا ما كان يعبد آباؤهم وإخوانهم. أما قس فاعتزل قومه، وعبد الله وحده، وكان يخطب على الناس ويأمرهم بالتوحيد، ويعيب عليهم عبادة الأصنام. وعاش سبعمائة عام. وأما زيدٌ فتعلق بالحنيفية، دين إبراهيم، حتى مات قبل البعثة. وأما ورقة- فأخذ بدين النصرانية التي لم تُغَيَّر، وأدرك أول البعثة، وآمن بالرسول قبل أن يُؤمر بالإنذار. قال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «رأيتُه في الجنة عليه ثيابُ خُضر» .\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: لولا فضل الله عليكم بأن بعث لكم مَن يدلكم على الله ويعرفكم بالله، ورحمته بأن أخرجكم من ضيق الفرق، إلى فضاء الجمع، لا تبعتم الفرق علمًا وعملاً، لكن الله تعالى بفضله ورحمته غيبكم عن شهود الفرق بشهود الملك الحق. إلاّ فرقًا قليلاً تقيمون به رسم العبودية، وتظهرون به الآداب مع الربوبية.\nقال الورتجبي: الفضل والرحمة منه للعموم، ومحبته للخصوص، الذين هم مستثنون بقوله: «إلا قليلاً» . هـ. قال القشيري: وَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ مع أوليائه لهاموا في كل وادٍ من التفرقة كأشكالهم في الوقت. هـ. فَخَصَّ الإشارة بالأولياء، وعليه فقوله: إِلَّا قَلِيلًا أي: إلا تفرقة قليلة تعرض لهم، تربيةً لهم، وإبقاء لرسمهم ومناطِ تكليفهم.\nوالله تعالى أعلم. قاله في الحاشية.\nولا يظهر هذا كله إلا بالجهاد الأكبر والأصغر، كما قال تعالى:","quran_ar":"وَإِذا جاءَهُمْ أَمْرٌ مِنَ الْأَمْنِ أَوِ الْخَوْفِ أَذاعُوا بِهِ وَلَوْ رَدُّوهُ إِلَى الرَّسُولِ وَإِلى أُولِي الْأَمْرِ مِنْهُمْ لَعَلِمَهُ الَّذِينَ يَسْتَنْبِطُونَهُ مِنْهُمْ وَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَتُهُ لاتَّبَعْتُمُ الشَّيْطانَ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً (83)","quran_en":"“When news concerning safety or fear comes to them, they broadcast it. Had they referred it to the Messenger and to those entrusted with authority among them, those capable of drawing it out from among them would have known it. Were it not for God's bounty toward you and His mercy, you would have followed Satan, except for a few.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Language and construction (*qultu*)","html":"<p><em>Istinbaṭa<\/em> means to extract or draw something out of another thing. The underlying image is taking out <em>nabaṭ<\/em>—water issuing from a well when it is first dug. The phrase “from among them” (<em>minhum<\/em>) may explain the relative pronoun: “those who could extract it, namely the holders of authority.” Alternatively it depends on “would have known”: the people who draw the information out for others would have known it <em>from the holders of authority<\/em>.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Istinbaṭa means to extract or draw something out of another thing. The underlying image is taking out nabaṭ —water issuing from a well when it is first dug. The phrase “from among them” ( minhum ) may explain the relative pronoun: “those who could extract it, namely the holders of authority.” Alternatively it depends on “would have known”: the people who draw the information out for others would have known it from the holders of authority ."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The outward interpretation: broadcasting without discernment","html":"<p>God censures hypocrites, or weaker Muslims, who receive news of military detachments that have gone out on campaigns: tidings of victory, booty and safety, or of danger, killing and defeat. Immediately they publicize the information, spreading alarming rumours before it has reached the Messenger and the senior Companions. These people of discernment (<em>ahl al-baṣāʾir<\/em>), who are holders of authority (<em>ulū l-amr<\/em>), know when and how such matters should be communicated. Had the news-bearers reported it privately to the Prophet, or kept silence until it came to him, or referred it to the senior Companions, those capable of drawing its real significance from the reports would have known what to convey. They would have communicated it in its proper form, without spreading rumour or frightening people. Alternatively the verse means that these competent authorities would first establish its meaning and only then tell the public, so that neither intimidation nor lack of propriety would result.<\/p>\n<p>There is another possible reference. News may come from heavenly revelation, bearing warning or reassurance, and be circulated before the Prophet himself has announced it. Had people kept silent and left it to the Messenger to proclaim, and to the leading Companions to explain, those able to derive its meaning from its source—the Messenger and his senior companions—would have known and communicated it properly. Ibn ʿAjība offers the incident of ʿUmar: on hearing that the Prophet had divorced his wives, he entered and asked, “Have you divorced your wives?” The Prophet replied, “No.” ʿUmar then stood at the mosque entrance and announced that the Messenger had <strong>not<\/strong> divorced them. He connected the revelation of this verse to that event and said, “I was the one who drew it out (<em>istanbaṭtuhu<\/em>).” God knows best.<\/p><p class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s source note:<\/strong> The central first-person narrative, including the direct question, public announcement and connection with 4:83, is preserved in Muslim 1479a. This source identification is an editorial observation.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God censures hypocrites, or weaker Muslims, who receive news of military detachments that have gone out on campaigns: tidings of victory, booty and safety, or of danger, killing and defeat. Immediately they publicize the information, spreading alarming rumours before it has reached the Messenger and the senior Companions. These people of discernment ( ahl al-baṣāʾir ), who are holders of authority ( ulū l-amr ), know when and how such matters should be communicated. Had the news-bearers reported it privately to the Prophet, or kept silence until it came to him, or referred it to the senior Companions, those capable of drawing its real significance from the reports would have known what to convey. They would have communicated it in its proper form, without spreading rumour or frightening people. Alternatively the verse means that these competent authorities would first establish its meaning and only then tell the public, so that neither intimidation nor lack of propriety would result. There is another possible reference. News may come from heavenly revelation, bearing warning or reassurance, and be circulated before the Prophet himself has announced it. Had people kept silent and left it to the Messenger to proclaim, and to the leading Companions to explain, those able to derive its meaning from its source—the Messenger and his senior companions—would have known and communicated it properly. Ibn ʿAjība offers the incident of ʿUmar: on hearing that the Prophet had divorced his wives, he entered and asked, “Have you divorced your wives?” The Prophet replied, “No.” ʿUmar then stood at the mosque entrance and announced that the Messenger had not divorced them. He connected the revelation of this verse to that event and said, “I was the one who drew it out ( istanbaṭtuhu ).” God knows best. Translator’s source note: The central first-person narrative, including the direct question, public announcement and connection with 4:83, is preserved in Muslim 1479a. This source identification is an editorial observation."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"the grave of secrets","html":"<p>The sages say, “The hearts of free people are the graves of secrets” (<em>qulūb al-aḥrār qubūr al-asrār<\/em>). The fault reproved here is widespread: some people, hearing something favourable or unfavourable, hasten to disclose it—especially when the story touches those affiliated with a spiritual way (<em>ahl al-nisba<\/em>) or those specially favoured (<em>ahl al-khuṣūṣiyya<\/em>). It may also arise among the <em>fuqarāʾ<\/em>. Ibn ʿAjība calls it heedlessness and a needless curiosity (<em>fuḍūl<\/em>). The truthful <em>faqīr<\/em> does not occupy himself with the passing reports of his time or with its people. He has left the people to whatever occupies them; absence in God may become so powerful that even the days pass beyond his notice. Someone who continually listens out for news and hunts it down possesses only the name of spiritual poverty without its reality.<\/p>\n<p>Some <em>fuqarāʾ<\/em>, he continues, bring their brothers' defects to the ears of their shaykhs, which can lead to expulsion from their company—God protect us. He quotes the Prophet as saying, “Do not bring me reports of the bad acts of my Companions,” because such news grieves the hearer. There is great good in bringing joy to the hearts of spiritual teachers. [Source note: Al-Tirmidhī 3896 transmits a longer report from Ibn Masʿūd containing the request, calls his route <em>gharīb<\/em>, and notes an additional intermediary in al-Walīd b. Abī Thawr’s transmission. Cairo’s “Do not bring me reports of the bad acts of my Companions” is an abbreviated wording.]<\/p>\n<p>The verse applies also to those who expose secrets of divine lordship (<em>asrār al-rubūbiyya<\/em>) and disclose to the <em>fuqarāʾ<\/em> matters of the inner reality (<em>ḥaqīqa<\/em>). It is better to refer the matter to the shaykh and let him disclose it. When <em>ḥaqīqa<\/em> is received from a teacher, a profound secret accompanies that transmission; this is not the case when it comes from someone else, except where that person has received the teacher's authorization (<em>idhn<\/em>), so that he acts, as it were, in his place. God knows best.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Al-Wartajbī, al-Kharrāz, and knowledge beyond the formulated report.<\/strong> Ibn ʿAjība then quotes Rūzbihān al-Wartajbī, who quotes Abū Saʿīd al-Kharrāz: God has servants upon whom a defect or disturbance enters; without it they would become spoiled and remain idle. They have reached an extreme degree in knowledge and arrived at knowledge of what is not formulated in a revealed book or transmitted account (<em>ʿilm al-majhūl<\/em>), but discerning knowers can still argue for it from the Book and sunna through subtle derivation (<em>ḥusn al-istinbāṭ<\/em>) and realized understanding. Al-Kharrāz invokes “those who draw it out among them would know it” [4:83].<\/p>\n<p>Ibn ʿAjība comments upon this as follows. God has occupied the scholars of outward learning (<em>ʿulamāʾ al-ẓāhir<\/em>) with establishing the science of differentiation (<em>ʿilm al-farq<\/em>). Without that occupation they would become idle, for, in his characterization, they do not possess the ability to practise the inward work of hearts—reflection (<em>fikra<\/em>) and contemplative looking (<em>naẓra<\/em>). Yet the knowers recognise and justify their undertaking by the scriptural rewards attached to teaching and disseminating knowledge. These scholars maintain the ordering of the science of wisdom (<em>ʿilm al-ḥikma<\/em>) and keep the banner of the <em>sharīʿa<\/em> upright. Were they not to do it, the people of inward knowing would have to perform that task, and their hearts would become disturbed by it. Ibn ʿAjība recalls the approximate words of the teacher of his teachers, Sīdī ʿAlī al-Jamal al-ʿImrānī: “May God reward them on our behalf with goodness! They have held high for us the banner of the <em>sharīʿa<\/em>. We plunge into the sea, and then raise our heads and see that the banner is still standing, then return to the sea.” He expressly says that he reports the sense, not a verbatim quotation. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"The sages say, “The hearts of free people are the graves of secrets” ( qulūb al-aḥrār qubūr al-asrār ). The fault reproved here is widespread: some people, hearing something favourable or unfavourable, hasten to disclose it—especially when the story touches those affiliated with a spiritual way ( ahl al-nisba ) or those specially favoured ( ahl al-khuṣūṣiyya ). It may also arise among the fuqarāʾ . Ibn ʿAjība calls it heedlessness and a needless curiosity ( fuḍūl ). The truthful faqīr does not occupy himself with the passing reports of his time or with its people. He has left the people to whatever occupies them; absence in God may become so powerful that even the days pass beyond his notice. Someone who continually listens out for news and hunts it down possesses only the name of spiritual poverty without its reality. Some fuqarāʾ , he continues, bring their brothers' defects to the ears of their shaykhs, which can lead to expulsion from their company—God protect us. He quotes the Prophet as saying, “Do not bring me reports of the bad acts of my Companions,” because such news grieves the hearer. There is great good in bringing joy to the hearts of spiritual teachers. [Source note: Al-Tirmidhī 3896 transmits a longer report from Ibn Masʿūd containing the request, calls his route gharīb , and notes an additional intermediary in al-Walīd b. Abī Thawr’s transmission. Cairo’s “Do not bring me reports of the bad acts of my Companions” is an abbreviated wording.] The verse applies also to those who expose secrets of divine lordship ( asrār al-rubūbiyya ) and disclose to the fuqarāʾ matters of the inner reality ( ḥaqīqa ). It is better to refer the matter to the shaykh and let him disclose it. When ḥaqīqa is received from a teacher, a profound secret accompanies that transmission; this is not the case when it comes from someone else, except where that person has received the teacher's authorization ( idhn ), so that he acts, as it were, in his place. God knows best. Al-Wartajbī, al-Kharrāz, and knowledge beyond the formulated report. Ibn ʿAjība then quotes Rūzbihān al-Wartajbī, who quotes Abū Saʿīd al-Kharrāz: God has servants upon whom a defect or disturbance enters; without it they would become spoiled and remain idle. They have reached an extreme degree in knowledge and arrived at knowledge of what is not formulated in a revealed book or transmitted account ( ʿilm al-majhūl ), but discerning knowers can still argue for it from the Book and sunna through subtle derivation ( ḥusn al-istinbāṭ ) and realized understanding. Al-Kharrāz invokes “those who draw it out among them would know it” [4:83]. Ibn ʿAjība comments upon this as follows. God has occupied the scholars of outward learning ( ʿulamāʾ al-ẓāhir ) with establishing the science of differentiation ( ʿilm al-farq ). Without that occupation they would become idle, for, in his characterization, they do not possess the ability to practise the inward work of hearts—reflection ( fikra ) and contemplative looking ( naẓra ). Yet the knowers recognise and justify their undertaking by the scriptural rewards attached to teaching and disseminating knowledge. These scholars maintain the ordering of the science of wisdom ( ʿilm al-ḥikma ) and keep the banner of the sharīʿa upright. Were they not to do it, the people of inward knowing would have to perform that task, and their hearts would become disturbed by it. Ibn ʿAjība recalls the approximate words of the teacher of his teachers, Sīdī ʿAlī al-Jamal al-ʿImrānī: “May God reward them on our behalf with goodness! They have held high for us the banner of the sharīʿa . We plunge into the sea, and then raise our heads and see that the banner is still standing, then return to the sea.” He expressly says that he reports the sense, not a verbatim quotation. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Guidance belongs to God. He establishes some in differentiation (<em>farq<\/em>) and guides others into gathered unity (<em>jamʿ<\/em>).<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Guidance belongs to God. He establishes some in differentiation ( farq ) and guides others into gathered unity ( jamʿ )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"God’s bounty and those guided before the mission","html":"<p><strong>“Were it not for God's bounty…”<\/strong> If God had not bestowed upon the community the Prophet of mercy, rescuing people from the pursuit of Satan and the worship of idols, they would have remained in unbelief and wandering and continued following Satan in unbelief and sin. The exception, in one outward explanation, concerns a small number who found guidance before the prophetic mission: Quss ibn Sāʿida, Zayd ibn Nufayl and Waraqa ibn Nawfal. God endowed them with exceptional understanding, so they reasoned and reflected until they recognised God and kept apart from what their families worshipped. Ibn ʿAjība relates that Quss withdrew from his people, worshipped God alone, preached divine unity and reproved idol-worship, and that he lived <strong>seven hundred years<\/strong>. <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> The seven-hundred-year figure is the author’s transmitted historical claim; it has not been independently corroborated.<\/span> Zayd adhered to the <em>ḥanīfiyya<\/em>, the religion of Abraham, until he died before the mission. Waraqa, in Ibn ʿAjība's description, followed an uncorrupted Christianity, lived to the start of the mission and believed in the Messenger before he was ordered to issue public warning. Ibn ʿAjība cites the Prophet as saying that he saw Waraqa in Paradise dressed in green garments. <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s source note:<\/strong> The green garments occur in Ibn Abī Shayba, <em>al-Muṣannaf<\/em> 37710, in a mursal narrative from Abū Maysara. A related version in al-Bayhaqī’s <em>Dalāʾil<\/em> II:158 has silk garments and is explicitly called disconnected by its compiler. Al-Tirmidhī 2288 gives white garments through a different route, which he calls gharīb while criticizing a transmitter. These witnesses establish the source history without warranting a change to Cairo’s green.<\/span><\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"“Were it not for God's bounty…” If God had not bestowed upon the community the Prophet of mercy, rescuing people from the pursuit of Satan and the worship of idols, they would have remained in unbelief and wandering and continued following Satan in unbelief and sin. The exception, in one outward explanation, concerns a small number who found guidance before the prophetic mission: Quss ibn Sāʿida, Zayd ibn Nufayl and Waraqa ibn Nawfal. God endowed them with exceptional understanding, so they reasoned and reflected until they recognised God and kept apart from what their families worshipped. Ibn ʿAjība relates that Quss withdrew from his people, worshipped God alone, preached divine unity and reproved idol-worship, and that he lived seven hundred years . Translator’s note: The seven-hundred-year figure is the author’s transmitted historical claim; it has not been independently corroborated. Zayd adhered to the ḥanīfiyya , the religion of Abraham, until he died before the mission. Waraqa, in Ibn ʿAjība's description, followed an uncorrupted Christianity, lived to the start of the mission and believed in the Messenger before he was ordered to issue public warning. Ibn ʿAjība cites the Prophet as saying that he saw Waraqa in Paradise dressed in green garments. Translator’s source note: The green garments occur in Ibn Abī Shayba, al-Muṣannaf 37710, in a mursal narrative from Abū Maysara. A related version in al-Bayhaqī’s Dalāʾil II:158 has silk garments and is explicitly called disconnected by its compiler. Al-Tirmidhī 2288 gives white garments through a different route, which he calls gharīb while criticizing a transmitter. These witnesses establish the source history without warranting a change to Cairo’s green."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"on bounty and unity","html":"<p>Were it not for God's bounty in sending you someone who points you toward Him and makes Him known, and His mercy in leading you out of the narrowness of differentiation into the openness of gathering (<em>faḍāʾ al-jamʿ<\/em>), you would follow separation in both knowledge and action. But through His bounty and mercy He has concealed the differentiated appearances from your vision by granting you witnessing of the True King. Only a little <em>farq<\/em> remains by which you maintain the visible form of servanthood (<em>rasm al-ʿubūdiyya<\/em>) and preserve due propriety before lordship (<em>adab maʿa l-rubūbiyya<\/em>).<\/p>\n<p>Al-Wartajbī says that divine bounty and mercy extend to people in general, while God's special love belongs to the elect—the “few” exempted in the verse. Al-Qushayrī says that but for divine bounty God's friends would wander through every valley of dispersion like others in their own day. He thus construes the verse allusively with regard to the saints. On this reading “except a few” means the small amount of differentiated awareness that overtakes them for their training, safeguarding their outward human form and preserving the locus on which legal responsibility (<em>taklīf<\/em>) rests. Ibn ʿAjība notes that this last interpretation comes from the marginal commentary. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Were it not for God's bounty in sending you someone who points you toward Him and makes Him known, and His mercy in leading you out of the narrowness of differentiation into the openness of gathering ( faḍāʾ al-jamʿ ), you would follow separation in both knowledge and action. But through His bounty and mercy He has concealed the differentiated appearances from your vision by granting you witnessing of the True King. Only a little farq remains by which you maintain the visible form of servanthood ( rasm al-ʿubūdiyya ) and preserve due propriety before lordship ( adab maʿa l-rubūbiyya ). Al-Wartajbī says that divine bounty and mercy extend to people in general, while God's special love belongs to the elect—the “few” exempted in the verse. Al-Qushayrī says that but for divine bounty God's friends would wander through every valley of dispersion like others in their own day. He thus construes the verse allusively with regard to the saints. On this reading “except a few” means the small amount of differentiated awareness that overtakes them for their training, safeguarding their outward human form and preserving the locus on which legal responsibility ( taklīf ) rests. Ibn ʿAjība notes that this last interpretation comes from the marginal commentary. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>These realizations become manifest only through the greater and lesser struggles (<em>al-jihād al-akbar wa-l-aṣghar<\/em>), the subject of the next verses.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"These realizations become manifest only through the greater and lesser struggles ( al-jihād al-akbar wa-l-aṣghar ), the subject of the next verses."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C536.1","page":536,"ar":"الحديث لم أقف عليه بهذا اللفظ، وورد عنه صلى الله عليه وسلم: «لا يبلغني أحد من أصحابي عن أحد شيئًا فإني أحب أن أخرج إليكم سليم الصدر». أخرجه أبو داود في (الأدب، باب رفع الحديث من المجلس) والترمذي في (المناقب، باب فضل أزواج النبي) من حديث ابن مسعود.","en":"I have not found the report in this wording. The following is related from him, God bless him and grant him peace: “Let none of my Companions convey anything about anyone to me, for I like to come out to you with an untroubled heart.” Abū Dāwūd records it in the Book of Conduct, chapter on conveying reports from a gathering, and al-Tirmidhī in the Book of Merits, chapter on the merits of the Prophet’s wives, from the report of Ibn Masʿūd.","anchor":"لا تبلغونى مساوئ أصحابي"}],"english_characters":9592,"arabic_characters":5078},{"id":"q4_cont_084","q":"4:84–86","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:84–86 · Striving, intercession, and the ethics of returning greetings","source":"Cairo I:538–540","cairo":"I:538–540","source_page_first":538,"source_page_last":540,"ar":"فَقاتِلْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ لا تُكَلَّفُ إِلاَّ نَفْسَكَ وَحَرِّضِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ عَسَى اللَّهُ أَنْ يَكُفَّ بَأْسَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وَاللَّهُ أَشَدُّ بَأْساً وَأَشَدُّ تَنْكِيلاً (84) مَنْ يَشْفَعْ شَفاعَةً حَسَنَةً يَكُنْ لَهُ نَصِيبٌ مِنْها وَمَنْ يَشْفَعْ شَفاعَةً سَيِّئَةً يَكُنْ لَهُ كِفْلٌ مِنْها وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ مُقِيتاً (85) وَإِذا حُيِّيتُمْ بِتَحِيَّةٍ فَحَيُّوا بِأَحْسَنَ مِنْها أَوْ رُدُّوها إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ حَسِيباً (86)\nقلت: (نفسك) : مفعول ثانٍ، والأول نائب، أي: لا يكلفك الله إلا نفسك.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فَقاتِلْ يا محمد فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ ولو وَحدَكَ إن تثبطوا عن الجهاد، لا نكلفك إلا أمر نفسك، وَلكن حَرِّضِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ على الجهاد، إذ ما عليك إلا التحريض. فجاهدوا حتى تكون كلمة الله هي العليا. عَسَى اللَّهُ أَنْ يَكُفَّ بجهادكم بَأْسَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا ويبطل دينهم الفاسد. وَاللَّهُ أَشَدُّ بَأْساً منهم وَأَشَدُّ تَنْكِيلًا أي: تعذيبًا لهم. وقد حقَّق الله ذلك ففتح الله على نبيه قبائل العرب، فلم يبق فيهم مشرك، ثم فتح على الصحابة سائر البلاد، وهدى الله بهم جميع العباد، إلا من فرّ من الكفار إلى شواهق الجبال.\nوإنما أمرتك بالتحريض على الجهاد لأن الدَّالُّ عَلَى الخَيْرِ كَفَاعِلِه، وذلك كالشفاعة بين الناس ودلالتهم على إصلاح ذات البين، فمن يَشْفَعْ شَفاعَةً حَسَنَةً بأن ينفع المشفوع له، بدفع ضرر أو جلب نفع، ابتغاء وجه الله، يَكُنْ لَهُ نَصِيبٌ مِنْها، أي: حظ كبير من الثواب لأنه دل المشفوع عنده على الخير، وأوصل النفع إلى المشفوع له، فله من الأجر مثل ما لهما، ومنها: الدعاء بظهر الغيب، فقد قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «مَن دعا لمسلمٍ بظَهرِ الغَيبِ استُجيب له، وقال له الملك: لك مِثلُ ذَلِكَ» .\nوَمَنْ يَشْفَعْ شَفاعَةً سَيِّئَةً، يريد بها فسادًا بين الناس كنميمة وزور وإحداث بدعة، يَكُنْ لَهُ كِفْلٌ أي:\nنصيب مِنْها أي: من وِزرها، وفي الحديث: «مَن سنَّ سُنةَ حسنة، فله أجرُهَا وأجرُ مَن عمل بها إلى يَومِ\nالقيامَةِ، ومن سنَّ سُنَّة سَيِّئةً فعليه وزرُهَا وَوِزرُ من عمل بها إلى يَوم القيامَةِ» . وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ مُقِيتاً أي: مقتدرًا من أقات على الشيء: إذَا قدر عليه، أو شهيدًا حافظَا فيجازي على قدر الأعمال.\nومن هذا أيضًا: السلام، فإنه سبب في ثواب الرد، لذلك ذكره الحق في سلك الدلالة على الخير فقال: وَإِذا حُيِّيتُمْ بِتَحِيَّةٍ فَحَيُّوا بِأَحْسَنَ مِنْها بأن تقولوا: وعليكم السلام والرحمة والبركة، أَوْ رُدُّوها بأن تقولوا:\nوعليكم السلام.\nوفي الخبر: «مَن قَالَ لأخيه المسلم: السَّلامُ علَيكُم، كتب الله له عَشرُ حَسنات، فإن قال: السَّلامُ عليكُم ورحمَةُ الله، كتب الله له عِشرين حَسنة، فإن قال: وَبَرَكَاتُه، كتب الله ثَلاثين» ، وكذلك لمن ردّ، فإن اقتصر على السلام، فعشر، وهكذا.. فإن ذكرَ المسلم الرحمةَ والبركةَ، قال الرادُّ: وعليكم، فقط، إذ لم يبق ما يزاد، ورد السلام واجب على الكفاية، حيث يكون مشروعًا، فلا يرد في الخطبة، وقراءة القرآن، والذكر والتفكر، والاعتبار، ونظرة الشهود والاستبصار، لأنه يفتر ويشوش، وفي الحمام إذا كانوا عراة، وفي حال الجماع والأكل والشرب وغيرها من المسائل المستثناة. وقد نظمه بعضهم، فقال:\nرَدُّ السَّلآم واجبٌ إلا على ... مَن في الصَّلاةِ أو بأكل شُغلا\nأو شُربٍ أو قراءةٍ أو أدعِيه ... أو ذكرٍ أو خُطبةٍ أو تَلبِيه\nوالسلام من تحية أهل الإسلام، خاصٌّ بهم. لذلك استغرب الخضر- عليه السلام- سلام سَيدنا موسى عليه السلام فقال له: «وأنَّى بأرضِكَ السَّلامُ» ، وكذلك خليل الله إبراهيم عليه السلام، إنما أنكر الملائكة حيث سلموا عليه بتحية أهل الإسلام لأنه كان بين أظهر قوم كفار، أما سلام أبي ذر على النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بتحية أهل الإسلام، قبل أن يسلم، فلعله سمعه من بعض الصحابة قبل أن يسلم، أو إلهام من الله. والله تعالى أعلم.\nإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ حَسِيباً يحاسبكم على التحية وغيرها. وبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: فجاهد أيها الإنسان نفسك في سبيل الله، لا تكلف إلا إصلاحها وتزكيتها، وحرض من يسمع قولك من المؤمنين على جهاد أنفسهم، عسى الله أن يكف عنهم القواطع والعلائق، فيتأهلون لإشراق قلوبهم بأنوار الحقائق، فإن الله لا يغلبه شيء، فمن ذكّر عبادَ الله، ودسهم إلى حضرة الله كان حظه كبيرًا عند الله. ومن دلهم على غير الله فقد غشهم وكان مُهانًا عند الله، وإذا وقع السلام على الفقراء فإن كانوا سالكين غير مشتغلين بالذكر\nوجب عليهم الرد بأحسن، وإذا كانوا ذاكرين أو متفكرين أو سكارى في شهود الحبيب سقط عنهم السلام، وكذلك إذا سلم عليهم اختبارًا وتعنيتًا لم يجب الرد. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولما ذكر أمر الحساب ذكر وقته، فقال:","quran_ar":"فَقاتِلْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ لا تُكَلَّفُ إِلاَّ نَفْسَكَ وَحَرِّضِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ عَسَى اللَّهُ أَنْ يَكُفَّ بَأْسَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وَاللَّهُ أَشَدُّ بَأْساً وَأَشَدُّ تَنْكِيلاً (84) مَنْ يَشْفَعْ شَفاعَةً حَسَنَةً يَكُنْ لَهُ نَصِيبٌ مِنْها وَمَنْ يَشْفَعْ شَفاعَةً سَيِّئَةً يَكُنْ لَهُ كِفْلٌ مِنْها وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ مُقِيتاً (85) وَإِذا حُيِّيتُمْ بِتَحِيَّةٍ فَحَيُّوا بِأَحْسَنَ مِنْها أَوْ رُدُّوها إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ حَسِيباً (86)","quran_en":"“Fight in God's way; you are responsible for none but yourself, and encourage the believers. Perhaps God will restrain the force of those who disbelieve. God is stronger in force and more severe in punishment. Whoever makes a good intercession shall have a share of it; whoever makes an evil intercession shall bear a portion of it. God has always had power over every thing. And when you are greeted with a greeting, respond with one better than it or return it in kind. God has always been a Reckoner over every thing.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p><em>Nafsaka<\/em> (“yourself”) is a second object, while the first functions as the deputy subject of the passive verb, understood as “you”. The underlying sense is: “God charges you with none but your own self.”<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Nafsaka (“yourself”) is a second object, while the first functions as the deputy subject of the passive verb, understood as “you”. The underlying sense is: “God charges you with none but your own self.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The outward interpretation: duty and encouraging others","html":"<p>God tells Muḥammad to fight in His way even alone if others hang back from the struggle: he has been charged with the duty pertaining to his own self. He should nevertheless encourage the believers, for encouragement is what is incumbent upon him with regard to them. Their struggle aims to make God's word uppermost. God may thereby ward off the power of the unbelievers and overturn the false religion to which they cling. God is stronger in warfare and sterner in chastisement than any opponent. Ibn ʿAjība sees this promise fulfilled in the Prophet's triumph over the tribes of Arabia, the disappearance of idolatry from among them, and the later extension of Muslim rule by the Companions across other lands, guiding inhabitants except those he says withdrew to inaccessible mountain heights. [Translator’s note: These are the commentator's retrospective historical generalizations, not a critical survey of the conquests.]<\/p>\n<p>The command to encourage others is joined to the maxim that “one who directs to good is like the one who does it.” Such direction includes beneficial intercession between people and guidance toward the restoration of good relations. Whoever intercedes well on someone's behalf—in order to avert harm or secure a benefit, seeking God's face—receives a substantial share of reward, for he has directed the person addressed toward good and conveyed benefit to the beneficiary. In recompense he receives a reward like theirs. Another instance is supplication for an absent person; Ibn ʿAjība quotes a report that one who prays for a Muslim in his absence is answered, and an angel says, “And the like of that for you.”<\/p>\n<p>Whoever makes a bad intercession, conversely, intending corruption among people through malicious tale-bearing, false witness or the institution of blameworthy innovation, receives a <em>kifl<\/em>, a portion of the resulting burden. Ibn ʿAjība cites the prophetic saying that one who introduces a good precedent (<em>sunna ḥasana<\/em>) receives its recompense and that of those who act upon it until the Resurrection, while one who initiates an evil precedent bears its burden and that of those who follow it. God's being <em>muqīt<\/em> over everything may mean that He has power over it (from <em>aqāta<\/em>, “to be able”), or that He watches and preserves it, recompensing actions in due measure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Greetings as an instance of good mediation.<\/strong> Peaceful greeting becomes an occasion for the reward earned by the one who responds; therefore it is mentioned alongside directing people to good. When greeted, one may reply with a more complete formula, “And upon you be peace, God's mercy and His blessing,” or with one equivalent to the original, “And upon you be peace.” Ibn ʿAjība quotes a report according to which “Peace be upon you” brings ten good deeds, adding “and God's mercy” brings twenty, and adding “and His blessings” brings thirty. The responder receives equivalent recompense. When the first speaker has already mentioned both mercy and blessing, Ibn ʿAjība's suggested response is simply “And upon you,” since no further element remains to add.<\/p>\n<p>He says that answering the greeting is a collective obligation (<em>farḍ ʿalā l-kifāya<\/em>) where the greeting is legally appropriate. He lists occasions in which, in his account of the law and Sufi propriety, a reply is not due: during a sermon, Qurʾān recitation, remembrance, reflection, spiritual consideration or contemplative witnessing (because a greeting would interrupt or unsettle the person); in a bathhouse when people are undressed; during sexual intercourse, eating or drinking; and other exceptional cases. He quotes instructional verse:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Returning peace is obligatory—save for one<br>engaged in prayer, or occupied with eating,<br>or drinking, reciting, supplicating,<br>remembering, preaching, or reciting the <em>talbiya<\/em>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>[Translator’s note: These are <strong>Ibn ʿAjība's stated exemptions<\/strong>; their scope differs among jurists and is not offered here as a settled universal legal ruling.] He describes <em>salām<\/em> as the distinctive greeting of the people of Islam. Thus, he says, al-Khiḍr was surprised when Moses greeted him with peace and asked, “How does peace come to be in your land?” Likewise, he explains Abraham's surprise at the angels' greeting by reference to the unbelieving people among whom he lived. As for Abū Dharr greeting the Prophet in this Islamic manner before he himself became Muslim, Ibn ʿAjība suggests that he heard the expression from some Companions or received it through divine prompting.<\/p>\n<p>“God is Reckoner over all things”: He calls people to account concerning greetings and every other matter. With God is success.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God tells Muḥammad to fight in His way even alone if others hang back from the struggle: he has been charged with the duty pertaining to his own self. He should nevertheless encourage the believers, for encouragement is what is incumbent upon him with regard to them. Their struggle aims to make God's word uppermost. God may thereby ward off the power of the unbelievers and overturn the false religion to which they cling. God is stronger in warfare and sterner in chastisement than any opponent. Ibn ʿAjība sees this promise fulfilled in the Prophet's triumph over the tribes of Arabia, the disappearance of idolatry from among them, and the later extension of Muslim rule by the Companions across other lands, guiding inhabitants except those he says withdrew to inaccessible mountain heights. [Translator’s note: These are the commentator's retrospective historical generalizations, not a critical survey of the conquests.] The command to encourage others is joined to the maxim that “one who directs to good is like the one who does it.” Such direction includes beneficial intercession between people and guidance toward the restoration of good relations. Whoever intercedes well on someone's behalf—in order to avert harm or secure a benefit, seeking God's face—receives a substantial share of reward, for he has directed the person addressed toward good and conveyed benefit to the beneficiary. In recompense he receives a reward like theirs. Another instance is supplication for an absent person; Ibn ʿAjība quotes a report that one who prays for a Muslim in his absence is answered, and an angel says, “And the like of that for you.” Whoever makes a bad intercession, conversely, intending corruption among people through malicious tale-bearing, false witness or the institution of blameworthy innovation, receives a kifl , a portion of the resulting burden. Ibn ʿAjība cites the prophetic saying that one who introduces a good precedent ( sunna ḥasana ) receives its recompense and that of those who act upon it until the Resurrection, while one who initiates an evil precedent bears its burden and that of those who follow it. God's being muqīt over everything may mean that He has power over it (from aqāta , “to be able”), or that He watches and preserves it, recompensing actions in due measure. Greetings as an instance of good mediation. Peaceful greeting becomes an occasion for the reward earned by the one who responds; therefore it is mentioned alongside directing people to good. When greeted, one may reply with a more complete formula, “And upon you be peace, God's mercy and His blessing,” or with one equivalent to the original, “And upon you be peace.” Ibn ʿAjība quotes a report according to which “Peace be upon you” brings ten good deeds, adding “and God's mercy” brings twenty, and adding “and His blessings” brings thirty. The responder receives equivalent recompense. When the first speaker has already mentioned both mercy and blessing, Ibn ʿAjība's suggested response is simply “And upon you,” since no further element remains to add. He says that answering the greeting is a collective obligation ( farḍ ʿalā l-kifāya ) where the greeting is legally appropriate. He lists occasions in which, in his account of the law and Sufi propriety, a reply is not due: during a sermon, Qurʾān recitation, remembrance, reflection, spiritual consideration or contemplative witnessing (because a greeting would interrupt or unsettle the person); in a bathhouse when people are undressed; during sexual intercourse, eating or drinking; and other exceptional cases. He quotes instructional verse: Returning peace is obligatory—save for one engaged in prayer, or occupied with eating, or drinking, reciting, supplicating, remembering, preaching, or reciting the talbiya . [Translator’s note: These are Ibn ʿAjība's stated exemptions ; their scope differs among jurists and is not offered here as a settled universal legal ruling.] He describes salām as the distinctive greeting of the people of Islam. Thus, he says, al-Khiḍr was surprised when Moses greeted him with peace and asked, “How does peace come to be in your land?” Likewise, he explains Abraham's surprise at the angels' greeting by reference to the unbelieving people among whom he lived. As for Abū Dharr greeting the Prophet in this Islamic manner before he himself became Muslim, Ibn ʿAjība suggests that he heard the expression from some Companions or received it through divine prompting. “God is Reckoner over all things”: He calls people to account concerning greetings and every other matter. With God is success."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>“Strive, O human being, against your own self in God's way. You are responsible for its reformation and purification, and should encourage believers who hear you to contend with their selves.” God may then remove their impediments (<em>qawāṭiʿ<\/em>) and entangling attachments (<em>ʿalāʾiq<\/em>), preparing their hearts to shine with the lights of the realities (<em>anwār al-ḥaqāʾiq<\/em>), for nothing can overcome God. Whoever recalls God's servants to Him and conveys them toward His Presence acquires a great share with God; one who directs them away from God betrays their trust and is abased.<\/p>\n<p>If a greeting is offered to members of the Sufi way, those engaged in ordinary travelling along the path (<em>sālikūn<\/em>) and not occupied with remembrance should answer in a better form. If they are absorbed in remembrance, contemplation, or spiritual intoxication in witnessing the Beloved, Ibn ʿAjība holds that an answering greeting is not required. Nor, he says, is a response required when the greeting is given to test, provoke or torment them. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"“Strive, O human being, against your own self in God's way. You are responsible for its reformation and purification, and should encourage believers who hear you to contend with their selves.” God may then remove their impediments ( qawāṭiʿ ) and entangling attachments ( ʿalāʾiq ), preparing their hearts to shine with the lights of the realities ( anwār al-ḥaqāʾiq ), for nothing can overcome God. Whoever recalls God's servants to Him and conveys them toward His Presence acquires a great share with God; one who directs them away from God betrays their trust and is abased. If a greeting is offered to members of the Sufi way, those engaged in ordinary travelling along the path ( sālikūn ) and not occupied with remembrance should answer in a better form. If they are absorbed in remembrance, contemplation, or spiritual intoxication in witnessing the Beloved, Ibn ʿAjība holds that an answering greeting is not required. Nor, he says, is a response required when the greeting is given to test, provoke or torment them. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having spoken of reckoning (<em>ḥisāb<\/em>), the Qurʾān turns to the appointed time of reckoning.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Having spoken of reckoning ( ḥisāb ), the Qurʾān turns to the appointed time of reckoning."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":5935,"arabic_characters":4035},{"id":"q4_cont_087","q":"4:87","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:87 · The gathering on the Last Day and the unity of the seeker's intention","source":"Cairo I:540","cairo":"I:540","source_page_first":540,"source_page_last":540,"ar":"اللَّهُ لاَ إِلهَ إِلاَّ هُوَ لَيَجْمَعَنَّكُمْ إِلى يَوْمِ الْقِيامَةِ لا رَيْبَ فِيهِ وَمَنْ أَصْدَقُ مِنَ اللَّهِ حَدِيثاً (87)\nقلت: (الله) : مبتدأ، و (لا إله) : خبر، أو اعتراض، و (ليجمعنكم) : خبر، وهو أوفق بالسياق، و (لا ريب فيه) حال، أو صفة لمصدر، أي: جمعًا لا ريب فيه.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: اللَّهُ لاَ إِلهَ إِلَّا هُوَ أي: لا مستحق للعبادة إلا هو، والله لَيَجْمَعَنَّكُمْ أي:\nليحشرنكم من قبوركم إِلى يَوْمِ الْقِيامَةِ للحساب الذي وعدكم به، لا شك فيه، فهو وعد صادق، وَمَنْ أَصْدَقُ مِنَ اللَّهِ حَدِيثاً، أي: لا أحدَ أصدقُ من الله حديثًا، لأن الكذب نقص، وهو على الله محال.\nالإشارة: الحق تعالى واحد في ملكه، فلا يذوق وحدانيته إلا من كان واحدًا في قصده وهمه، فكل من وحَّد قلبه وقصده وهمته في طلبه، وانجمع بكليته إليه، جمعه الله لحضرته، ونعَّمه بشهود ذاته، وعداً حقاً وقولاً صدقاً، لا ريب فيه ولا اشتباه، إذ لا أحدَ أصدق من الله.\nثم رجع إلى الكلام مع المنافقين، فقال:","quran_ar":"اللَّهُ لاَ إِلهَ إِلاَّ هُوَ لَيَجْمَعَنَّكُمْ إِلى يَوْمِ الْقِيامَةِ لا رَيْبَ فِيهِ وَمَنْ أَصْدَقُ مِنَ اللَّهِ حَدِيثاً (87)","quran_en":"“God—there is no god but He—shall certainly gather you on the Day of Resurrection, about which there is no doubt. And who speaks more truthfully than God?”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>“God” (<em>Allāh<\/em>) is an initial subject (<em>mubtadaʾ<\/em>), with “there is no god” (<em>lā ilāha<\/em>) as predicate (<em>khabar<\/em>). Alternatively the declaration of divine unity is parenthetic, and “He will certainly gather you” (<em>la-yajmaʿannakum<\/em>) is the predicate; this more closely suits the context. “There is no doubt in it” (<em>lā rayba fīhi<\/em>) is either a circumstance (<em>ḥāl<\/em>) or an adjective qualifying an unexpressed verbal noun: “a gathering in which there is no doubt.”<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"“God” ( Allāh ) is an initial subject ( mubtadaʾ ), with “there is no god” ( lā ilāha ) as predicate ( khabar ). Alternatively the declaration of divine unity is parenthetic, and “He will certainly gather you” ( la-yajmaʿannakum ) is the predicate; this more closely suits the context. “There is no doubt in it” ( lā rayba fīhi ) is either a circumstance ( ḥāl ) or an adjective qualifying an unexpressed verbal noun: “a gathering in which there is no doubt.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>God alone deserves worship. By God, He will certainly raise you out of your graves and assemble you on the Day of Resurrection to face the accounting He has promised. There is no doubt about that day or His promise, for the promise is true. Who could speak more truthfully than God? No one, because lying is a defect, and defect is impossible of God.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God alone deserves worship. By God, He will certainly raise you out of your graves and assemble you on the Day of Resurrection to face the accounting He has promised. There is no doubt about that day or His promise, for the promise is true. Who could speak more truthfully than God? No one, because lying is a defect, and defect is impossible of God."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>The True (<em>al-Ḥaqq<\/em>) is one in His kingdom, and no one tastes His oneness except a person who becomes single in his aim and spiritual concern. Whoever gathers his heart, intention (<em>qaṣd<\/em>) and spiritual aspiration (<em>himma<\/em>) into a single pursuit of God, turning his entire being toward Him, God gathers him into His Presence and favours him with the witnessing of His Essence (<em>shuhūd dhātihi<\/em>). This is His real promise and truthful word, free from doubt and ambiguity. None is truer in speech than God.<\/p>\n<p>Ibn ʿAjība next returns to the matter of the hypocrites.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"The True ( al-Ḥaqq ) is one in His kingdom, and no one tastes His oneness except a person who becomes single in his aim and spiritual concern. Whoever gathers his heart, intention ( qaṣd ) and spiritual aspiration ( himma ) into a single pursuit of God, turning his entire being toward Him, God gathers him into His Presence and favours him with the witnessing of His Essence ( shuhūd dhātihi ). This is His real promise and truthful word, free from doubt and ambiguity. None is truer in speech than God. Ibn ʿAjība next returns to the matter of the hypocrites."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1370,"arabic_characters":871},{"id":"q4_cont_088","q":"4:88","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:88 · Disagreement over the hypocrites","source":"Cairo I:540","cairo":"I:540","source_page_first":540,"source_page_last":540,"ar":"فَما لَكُمْ فِي الْمُنافِقِينَ فِئَتَيْنِ وَاللَّهُ أَرْكَسَهُمْ بِما كَسَبُوا أَتُرِيدُونَ أَنْ تَهْدُوا مَنْ أَضَلَّ اللَّهُ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلِ اللَّهُ فَلَنْ تَجِدَ لَهُ سَبِيلاً (88)","quran_ar":"فَما لَكُمْ فِي الْمُنافِقِينَ فِئَتَيْنِ وَاللَّهُ أَرْكَسَهُمْ بِما كَسَبُوا أَتُرِيدُونَ أَنْ تَهْدُوا مَنْ أَضَلَّ اللَّهُ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلِ اللَّهُ فَلَنْ تَجِدَ لَهُ سَبِيلاً (88)","quran_en":"Why have you become two parties concerning the hypocrites, when God has cast them back on account of what they have earned? Do you wish to guide one whom God has led astray? For one whom God leads astray you will find no path.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>fiʾatayn<\/em> (“two parties”) is a circumstantial accusative (<em>ḥāl<\/em>); its governing expression is the implicit idea of being or remaining conveyed by the preceding prepositional phrase. To <em>arkasa<\/em> something is to turn it upside down or throw it back. God, exalted is His majesty, reproaches the Companions for dividing into two groups over the status of certain hypocrites, instead of agreeing concerning their unbelief. God had cast those people back into unbelief after they had displayed Islam, as the consequence of their sins. Do you propose to guide people whose wretched destiny God already knew? Whoever God leads astray will find no way to guidance.<\/p>\n<p>Ibn ʿAbbās is cited as saying that the verse concerned people in Mecca who claimed to have embraced Islam but did not emigrate. When some journeyed to Syria on business, Muslims differed: should they be killed and their merchandise taken because they had not emigrated, or spared because they professed belief? Another report concerns men who embraced Islam but found Medina unhealthy. They asked permission to return to the desert, and, once away, travelled on until they joined the polytheists; Muslims then disputed their religious status. [Translator’s note: These are reported occasions of revelation, not independent conclusions concerning contemporary persons.]<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: fiʾatayn (“two parties”) is a circumstantial accusative ( ḥāl ); its governing expression is the implicit idea of being or remaining conveyed by the preceding prepositional phrase. To arkasa something is to turn it upside down or throw it back. God, exalted is His majesty, reproaches the Companions for dividing into two groups over the status of certain hypocrites, instead of agreeing concerning their unbelief. God had cast those people back into unbelief after they had displayed Islam, as the consequence of their sins. Do you propose to guide people whose wretched destiny God already knew? Whoever God leads astray will find no way to guidance. Ibn ʿAbbās is cited as saying that the verse concerned people in Mecca who claimed to have embraced Islam but did not emigrate. When some journeyed to Syria on business, Muslims differed: should they be killed and their merchandise taken because they had not emigrated, or spared because they professed belief? Another report concerns men who embraced Islam but found Medina unhealthy. They asked permission to return to the desert, and, once away, travelled on until they joined the polytheists; Muslims then disputed their religious status. [Translator’s note: These are reported occasions of revelation, not independent conclusions concerning contemporary persons.]"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C541.1","page":541,"ar":"أي أصابهم الجوى: وهو المرض وداء الجوف إذا تطاول. وذلك إذ لم يوافقهم هواؤها، واستوخموها، ويقال: اجتويت البلد: إذا كرهت المقام فيه، وإن كنت في نعمة. انظر النهاية في غريب الحديث (جوا).","en":"That is, they were afflicted by jawā: illness and a prolonged internal ailment. This was because its air did not agree with them and they found it unhealthy. One also says, “I found the town disagreeable,” when one dislikes remaining there, even while enjoying comfortable circumstances. See al-Nihāya fī gharīb al-ḥadīth, under j-w-ā.","anchor":"اجتَوَوا المدينة"}],"english_characters":1328,"arabic_characters":183},{"id":"q4_cont_089","q":"4:89–90","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:89–90 · Emigration, hostility, and protected parties","source":"Cairo I:540–542","cairo":"I:540–542","source_page_first":540,"source_page_last":542,"ar":"وَدُّوا لَوْ تَكْفُرُونَ كَما كَفَرُوا فَتَكُونُونَ سَواءً ...\nقلت: (فئتين) : حال، والعامل فيه: الاستقرار في الجر، وأركس الشيء: نكَّسه.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله معاتبًا الصحابة حين اختلفوا في إسلام بعض المنافقين، فقال: فَما لَكُمْ افترقتم فِي شأن الْمُنافِقِينَ فرقتين، ولم تتفقوا على كفرهم، والحالة أن الله- تعالى- أَرْكَسَهُمْ، أي: نكَّسهم وردهم إلى الكفر بعد أن أظهروا الإسلام بسبب ما كسبوا من الآثام. أَتُرِيدُونَ أَنْ تَهْدُوا مَنْ أَضَلَّ اللَّهُ، وسبق لهم الشقاء في علم الله؟ ومَن يُضللِ اللهُ فلن تجد له طريقاً إلى الهدى. قال ابن عباس- رضى الله عنهما-: (نزلت في قوم كانوا بمكة من المشركين، فزعموا أنهم آمنوا ولم يهاجروا، ثم سافر قوم منهم بتجارات إلى الشام، فاختلف\nالمسلمون، هل يقتلونهم ليغنموا تجارتهم، لأنهم لم يهاجروا، أو يتركونهم لأنهم مؤمنون؟) . وقيل: في قوم أسلموا ثم اجتَوَوا المدينة (1) ، واستأذنوا رسول صلّى الله عليه وسلم في الخروج إلى البدو، فلما خَرَجُوا لم يزالُوا راحلين مَرحلةً حتى لحقُوا بالمُشركين، فاختلف المسلمون فى إسلامهم.\n\nثم حكم بكفرهم فقال وَدُّوا لَوْ تَكْفُرُونَ أي: يتمنون كفركم كَما كَفَرُوا فَتَكُونُونَ معهم سَواءً في الضلال والكفر.\nالإشارة: مَنْ دخل في طريق المخصوصين الأبرار، ثم لم تساعده رياح الأقدار، فلا ينبغي الكلام فيه، ولا الخوض في شأنه، لأن أمره بيد ربه، (من يهده الله فلا مضل له) ، ومن يضلل فلا ناصر له. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم نهى عن مُوَالاَتهم، فقال:\n... فَلا تَتَّخِذُوا مِنْهُمْ أَوْلِياءَ حَتَّى يُهاجِرُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ فَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا فَخُذُوهُمْ وَاقْتُلُوهُمْ حَيْثُ وَجَدْتُمُوهُمْ وَلا تَتَّخِذُوا مِنْهُمْ وَلِيًّا وَلا نَصِيراً (89) إِلاَّ الَّذِينَ يَصِلُونَ إِلى قَوْمٍ بَيْنَكُمْ وَبَيْنَهُمْ مِيثاقٌ أَوْ جاؤُكُمْ حَصِرَتْ صُدُورُهُمْ أَنْ يُقاتِلُوكُمْ أَوْ يُقاتِلُوا قَوْمَهُمْ وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ لَسَلَّطَهُمْ عَلَيْكُمْ فَلَقاتَلُوكُمْ فَإِنِ اعْتَزَلُوكُمْ فَلَمْ يُقاتِلُوكُمْ وَأَلْقَوْا إِلَيْكُمُ السَّلَمَ فَما جَعَلَ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ عَلَيْهِمْ سَبِيلاً (90)\nقلت: (حَصِرت) : أي: ضاقت، والجملة حال من الواو، بدليل قراءة يعقوب (حَصِرَةً) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فَلا تَتَّخِذُوا من هؤلاء الكفرة أَوْلِياءَ وأصدقاء حتى يتحقق إيمانهم، بأن يهاجروا من دار الكفر إلى دار الإسلام فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وابتغاء مرضات الله، لا لحرف دنيوي، فَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا عن إظهار الإيمان بالهجرة فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ، فَخُذُوهُمْ أسارى وَاقْتُلُوهُمْ حَيْثُ وَجَدْتُمُوهُمْ كسائر الكفرة، وجانبوهم وَلا تَتَّخِذُوا مِنْهُمْ وَلِيًّا وَلا نَصِيراً أي: لا تستعينوا بهم في جهادكم، إِلَّا الَّذِينَ يَصِلُونَ إِلى قَوْمٍ بَيْنَكُمْ وَبَيْنَهُمْ عهد، ومِيثاقٌ أي: مهادنة، فلهم حكم المُعَاهَدِين الذين وصلوا إليهم، ودخلوا معهم في الصلح، فلا تقتلوهم ولا تأسروهم.\nوكانت خزاعة وادعت النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم وعقدت معه الصلح، فجاء بنو مدلج فدخلوا معهم في الصلح، فنهى الله عن قتالهم ماداموا معهم، فالقوم الذين بين المسلمين وبينهم ميثاق هم خزاعة، والذين وصلوا إليهم هم بنو مدلج.\nفالاستثناء على هذا منقطع، لأن بني مدلج حينئٍذ كانت مظهرة للكفر لا منافقة، ويحتمل أن يكون متصلاً، أي: إلا الذين يصلون منهم ... الخ، فتأمل. وكان هذا في أول الإسلام، ثم نسخ بقوله: فَاقْتُلُوا الْمُشْرِكِينَ حَيْثُ وَجَدْتُمُوهُمْ الآية.\nثم ذكر قومًا آخرين نهى عن قتالهم، فقال: أَوْ جاؤُكُمْ أي: إلّا قوما جاءوكم، قد حَصِرَتْ صُدُورُهُمْ أي: ضاقت عن أَنْ يُقاتِلُوكُمْ أَوْ يُقاتِلُوا قَوْمَهُمْ يعني أنهم كرهوا قتالهم، وكرهوا قتال قومهم الكفار، فلا تقتلوهم أيضًا، لأن الله كفَّ شرهم عنكم، وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ لَسَلَّطَهُمْ عَلَيْكُمْ بأن قوَّى قلوبهم وأزال رعبهم فَلَقاتَلُوكُمْ ولم يكفّوا عنكم، فَإِنِ اعْتَزَلُوكُمْ ولم يتعرّضوا لكم وَأَلْقَوْا إِلَيْكُمُ السَّلَمَ أي: الاستسلام والانقياد فَما جَعَلَ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ عَلَيْهِمْ سَبِيلًا أي: طريقًا إلى قتالهم.\nالإشارة: نهى الله تعالى عن مساكنة النفوس وموالاتها، حتى تهاجر عن مواطن شهواتها إلى حضرة ربها، فإن تولت عن الهجرة وألِفَت البطالةَ والغفلة فليأخذها ليقتلها حيثما ظهرت صورتها، ولا يسكن إليها أبدًا أو يواليها، إلاَّ إن وصلت إلى حضرة الشيخ، وأمره بالرفق بها، أو كفت عن طغيانها، أو كفى الله أمرها بجذبٍ أخرجها عن عوائدها، أو واردٍ قوَّى دفع شهواتها، فإنه يأتي من حضرة قهّار، لا يصادم شيئاً إلا دمغه، وهذه عناية من الرحمن، ولو شاء الله تعالى لسلطها على الإنسان يرخى لها العنان، فتجمح به في ضَحضَاح النيران، فإن كفت النفس عن شهواتها، وانقادت إلى حضرة ربها، فما لأحدٍ عليها من سبيل، وقد دخلت في حمى الملك الجليل.\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر صنفا آخر من المنافقين، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَدُّوا لَوْ تَكْفُرُونَ كَما كَفَرُوا فَتَكُونُونَ سَواءً فَلا تَتَّخِذُوا مِنْهُمْ أَوْلِياءَ حَتَّى يُهاجِرُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ فَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا فَخُذُوهُمْ وَاقْتُلُوهُمْ حَيْثُ وَجَدْتُمُوهُمْ وَلا تَتَّخِذُوا مِنْهُمْ وَلِيًّا وَلا نَصِيراً (89) إِلاَّ الَّذِينَ يَصِلُونَ إِلى قَوْمٍ بَيْنَكُمْ وَبَيْنَهُمْ مِيثاقٌ أَوْ جاؤُكُمْ حَصِرَتْ صُدُورُهُمْ أَنْ يُقاتِلُوكُمْ أَوْ يُقاتِلُوا قَوْمَهُمْ وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ لَسَلَّطَهُمْ عَلَيْكُمْ فَلَقاتَلُوكُمْ فَإِنِ اعْتَزَلُوكُمْ فَلَمْ يُقاتِلُوكُمْ وَأَلْقَوْا إِلَيْكُمُ السَّلَمَ فَما جَعَلَ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ عَلَيْهِمْ سَبِيلاً (90)","quran_en":"They wish you to disbelieve as they disbelieve, so that you would be equal. Do not take them as protecting friends until they emigrate in God's way. If they turn back, seize and kill them wherever you encounter them, and take no protector or helper from among them—except those who reach a people with whom you have a covenant, or those who come to you with hearts constrained from fighting you or fighting their own people. Had God willed, He would have given them power over you and they would have fought you. If they withdraw, do not fight you, and offer you peace, then God has provided you no way against them.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Running commentary","html":"<p><strong>Continuation of the previous exposition.<\/strong> God describes those persons as wishing that the believers would become unbelievers like themselves, thus becoming their equals in error and unbelief.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Continuation of the previous exposition. God describes those persons as wishing that the believers would become unbelievers like themselves, thus becoming their equals in error and unbelief."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"to the previous verse","html":"<p>Whoever entered the path of the specially chosen and righteous but was not helped by the winds of decree should not become the subject of speculative talk or intrusive inquiry. His case rests with his Lord: no one can misguide a person God guides, and no one can assist a person left astray. God gives success and directs to the straight path.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever entered the path of the specially chosen and righteous but was not helped by the winds of decree should not become the subject of speculative talk or intrusive inquiry. His case rests with his Lord: no one can misguide a person God guides, and no one can assist a person left astray. God gives success and directs to the straight path."},{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>ḥaṣirat<\/em> means that their breasts were constricted. The sentence is circumstantial (<em>ḥāl<\/em>) in relation to the plural pronoun. This is corroborated by Yaʿqūb's reading <em>ḥaṣiratan<\/em> in adjectival form, “constricted.”<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ḥaṣirat means that their breasts were constricted. The sentence is circumstantial ( ḥāl ) in relation to the plural pronoun. This is corroborated by Yaʿqūb's reading ḥaṣiratan in adjectival form, “constricted.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>God prohibits taking the people just described as close allies and protectors before their faith is manifest in emigration from the abode of unbelief to the abode of Islam, for God's sake rather than for a worldly advantage. If they turn away from manifesting faith through emigration in God’s way, Ibn ʿAjība explains the command as: ‘Take them captive and kill them wherever you find them, like the other unbelievers.’ He adds that they are to be shunned. They are not to be relied upon as helpers in the believers' military expeditions. Yet people who attach themselves to a party with whom Muslims have a treaty share the protections of those bound by the treaty: they are neither to be killed nor taken captive.<\/p>\n<p>He identifies the treaty partners in one report as Khuzāʿa, with whom the Prophet made a peace agreement, and those joining them as Banū Mudlij. The command not to fight the latter held while they remained protected by that agreement. On this account the grammatical exception is disconnected (<em>istithnāʾ munqaṭiʿ<\/em>), since Banū Mudlij were openly unbelievers, not the hypocrites first mentioned. Alternatively it is connected, referring to those among the first group who joined a treaty-bound people. Ibn ʿAjība adds the traditional exegetical claim that the early rule was later abrogated by the verse “Kill the polytheists wherever you find them” (9:5). [Translator’s note: This is a report of his exegetical position; it is not an unrestricted legal instruction, and the conditions and debates over abrogation require independent juristic examination.]<\/p>\n<p>A second protected class consists of people who come unwilling either to fight the Muslims or to fight their own kin. Their hearts recoil from both engagements. They too are not to be fought, for God has restrained the harm they might otherwise have done. Had He removed their fear and strengthened their resolve, they could have fought. But if they keep apart, refrain from hostilities and offer peaceful submission or a peace agreement, there is no legitimate cause to attack them.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God prohibits taking the people just described as close allies and protectors before their faith is manifest in emigration from the abode of unbelief to the abode of Islam, for God's sake rather than for a worldly advantage. If they turn away from manifesting faith through emigration in God’s way, Ibn ʿAjība explains the command as: ‘Take them captive and kill them wherever you find them, like the other unbelievers.’ He adds that they are to be shunned. They are not to be relied upon as helpers in the believers' military expeditions. Yet people who attach themselves to a party with whom Muslims have a treaty share the protections of those bound by the treaty: they are neither to be killed nor taken captive. He identifies the treaty partners in one report as Khuzāʿa, with whom the Prophet made a peace agreement, and those joining them as Banū Mudlij. The command not to fight the latter held while they remained protected by that agreement. On this account the grammatical exception is disconnected ( istithnāʾ munqaṭiʿ ), since Banū Mudlij were openly unbelievers, not the hypocrites first mentioned. Alternatively it is connected, referring to those among the first group who joined a treaty-bound people. Ibn ʿAjība adds the traditional exegetical claim that the early rule was later abrogated by the verse “Kill the polytheists wherever you find them” (9:5). [Translator’s note: This is a report of his exegetical position; it is not an unrestricted legal instruction, and the conditions and debates over abrogation require independent juristic examination.] A second protected class consists of people who come unwilling either to fight the Muslims or to fight their own kin. Their hearts recoil from both engagements. They too are not to be fought, for God has restrained the harm they might otherwise have done. Had He removed their fear and strengthened their resolve, they could have fought. But if they keep apart, refrain from hostilities and offer peaceful submission or a peace agreement, there is no legitimate cause to attack them."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>The prohibition can also be applied to intimate accommodation of one's lower soul (<em>nafs<\/em>), until it emigrates from the dwelling places of its desires to the Presence of its Lord. If it turns away from this emigration and becomes accustomed to idleness and heedlessness, Ibn ʿAjība enjoins seizing and slaying it wherever its form appears, never settling with it or befriending it. Yet if it reaches the spiritual master's presence and he directs gentleness toward it, if it desists from tyranny, or if a divine attraction (<em>jadhb<\/em>) or incoming spiritual impulse (<em>wārid<\/em>) drives it out of its habits and repels its appetites, it should no longer be treated in that manner. Such help proceeds from the Presence of the All-Subduer: it strikes nothing without crushing it. This is care from the All-Merciful. Were God to give the soul unrestricted power, it would bolt toward the shallows of the Fire. Once it restrains its passions and yields to its Lord, no one has a cause against it: it has entered the sanctuary of the Sovereign. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"The prohibition can also be applied to intimate accommodation of one's lower soul ( nafs ), until it emigrates from the dwelling places of its desires to the Presence of its Lord. If it turns away from this emigration and becomes accustomed to idleness and heedlessness, Ibn ʿAjība enjoins seizing and slaying it wherever its form appears, never settling with it or befriending it. Yet if it reaches the spiritual master's presence and he directs gentleness toward it, if it desists from tyranny, or if a divine attraction ( jadhb ) or incoming spiritual impulse ( wārid ) drives it out of its habits and repels its appetites, it should no longer be treated in that manner. Such help proceeds from the Presence of the All-Subduer: it strikes nothing without crushing it. This is care from the All-Merciful. Were God to give the soul unrestricted power, it would bolt toward the shallows of the Fire. Once it restrains its passions and yields to its Lord, no one has a cause against it: it has entered the sanctuary of the Sovereign. God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3855,"arabic_characters":4157},{"id":"q4_cont_091","q":"4:91","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:91 · Those who seek protection from both sides","source":"Cairo I:542","cairo":"I:542–543","source_page_first":542,"source_page_last":543,"ar":"سَتَجِدُونَ آخَرِينَ يُرِيدُونَ أَنْ يَأْمَنُوكُمْ وَيَأْمَنُوا قَوْمَهُمْ كُلَّما رُدُّوا إِلَى الْفِتْنَةِ أُرْكِسُوا فِيها فَإِنْ لَمْ يَعْتَزِلُوكُمْ وَيُلْقُوا إِلَيْكُمُ السَّلَمَ وَيَكُفُّوا أَيْدِيَهُمْ فَخُذُوهُمْ وَاقْتُلُوهُمْ حَيْثُ ثَقِفْتُمُوهُمْ وَأُولئِكُمْ جَعَلْنا لَكُمْ عَلَيْهِمْ سُلْطاناً مُبِيناً (91)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: سَتَجِدُونَ قوماً آخَرِينَ منافقين، وهم أسد وغطفان، قَدِمُوا المدينة، وأظهروا الإسلام نفاقًا ورياء إذا لقوا النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم قالوا: إنا على دينك، يريدون الأمن، إذا لقوا قومهم، وقالوا لأحدهم: لماذا أسلمت، ومن تعبد؟ فيقول: لهذا القرد ولهذا العقرب والخنفساء، يُرِيدُونَ بإظهار الإسلام أَنْ يَأْمَنُوكُمْ وَيَأْمَنُوا قَوْمَهُمْ، كُلَّما رُدُّوا إِلَى الْفِتْنَةِ أُرْكِسُوا فِيها، أي: كلما دُعُوا إلى الكفر رَجَعُوا إليه أقبحَ رد، فَإِنْ لَمْ يَعْتَزِلُوكُمْ وَيُلْقُوا إِلَيْكُمُ السَّلَمَ أي: ولم يلقوا إليكم المسالمة والصلح، ولم يَكُفُّوا أَيْدِيَهُمْ بأن تعرضوا لكم فَخُذُوهُمْ وَاقْتُلُوهُمْ حَيْثُ ثَقِفْتُمُوهُمْ أي: وجدتموهم، وَأُولئِكُمْ جَعَلْنا لَكُمْ عَلَيْهِمْ سُلْطاناً، أي: تسلطاً مُبِيناً ظاهرًا، لظهور كفرهم وثبوت عداوتهم.\nالإشارة: النفوس على ثلاثة أقسام: قسم مطلقة العنان في الجرائم والعصيان، وهي النفوس الأمارة، وإليها الإشارة بالآية قبلها، والله أعلم. وقسم مذبذبة تارة تظهر الطاعة والإذعان، تريد أن يأمنها صاحبها، وتارة ترجع إلى الغي والعصيان، مهما دعيت إلى فتنة وقعت فيها، فإن لم تنته عن ذلك، وتكف عن غيها، فالواجب جهادها وقتلها حتى تنقاد بالكلية إلى ربها، وأما النفس المطمئنة فلا كلام معها لتحقق إسلامها، فالواجب الكف عنها وحبها. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولما فرغ من حفظ الأديان تكلم على بقية حفظ الأبدان، فقال:","quran_ar":"سَتَجِدُونَ آخَرِينَ يُرِيدُونَ أَنْ يَأْمَنُوكُمْ وَيَأْمَنُوا قَوْمَهُمْ كُلَّما رُدُّوا إِلَى الْفِتْنَةِ أُرْكِسُوا فِيها فَإِنْ لَمْ يَعْتَزِلُوكُمْ وَيُلْقُوا إِلَيْكُمُ السَّلَمَ وَيَكُفُّوا أَيْدِيَهُمْ فَخُذُوهُمْ وَاقْتُلُوهُمْ حَيْثُ ثَقِفْتُمُوهُمْ وَأُولئِكُمْ جَعَلْنا لَكُمْ عَلَيْهِمْ سُلْطاناً مُبِيناً (91)","quran_en":"You will find others who wish to feel secure with you and with their own people. Whenever they are returned to sedition, they are cast headlong into it. Unless they withdraw from you, offer peace and restrain their hands, seize and kill them wherever you encounter them. Against such people We have given you clear authority.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>God speaks of another class of hypocrites, identified in the report as men of Asad and Ghaṭafān. On coming to Medina they professed Islam and told the Prophet they followed his religion, seeking security. On returning to their kin, when asked why and whom they worshipped, one answered derisively that he worshipped a monkey, a scorpion and a beetle. Such persons sought safety with both sides. Each time they were summoned to unbelief they plunged into it more shamefully. If they refuse separation from hostilities, decline to offer peace and do not restrain their hands from attacking, the text commands, “Seize them and kill them wherever you find them,” and states that clear authority has been granted against them, because their unbelief is manifest and their hostility established. [Translator’s note: This interpretation is Ibn ʿAjība's description of the passage's historical combatants.]<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God speaks of another class of hypocrites, identified in the report as men of Asad and Ghaṭafān. On coming to Medina they professed Islam and told the Prophet they followed his religion, seeking security. On returning to their kin, when asked why and whom they worshipped, one answered derisively that he worshipped a monkey, a scorpion and a beetle. Such persons sought safety with both sides. Each time they were summoned to unbelief they plunged into it more shamefully. If they refuse separation from hostilities, decline to offer peace and do not restrain their hands from attacking, the text commands, “Seize them and kill them wherever you find them,” and states that clear authority has been granted against them, because their unbelief is manifest and their hostility established. [Translator’s note: This interpretation is Ibn ʿAjība's description of the passage's historical combatants.]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Lower souls fall into three classes. Some are wholly unrestrained in crime and disobedience—the commanding soul (<em>al-nafs al-ammāra<\/em>), alluded to by the preceding verses. Others vacillate: they display obedience and submission to reassure their possessor, then return to error and disobedience whenever temptation calls. So long as they refuse to desist, Ibn ʿAjība declares struggle against them and their “slaying” obligatory until they yield wholly to their Lord. As for the soul at peace (<em>al-nafs al-muṭmaʾinna<\/em>), there is no conflict with it: its submission has become real, and it must be left alone and loved. Having treated the safeguarding of religion, the commentary next turns to the safeguarding of human lives.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Lower souls fall into three classes. Some are wholly unrestrained in crime and disobedience—the commanding soul ( al-nafs al-ammāra ), alluded to by the preceding verses. Others vacillate: they display obedience and submission to reassure their possessor, then return to error and disobedience whenever temptation calls. So long as they refuse to desist, Ibn ʿAjība declares struggle against them and their “slaying” obligatory until they yield wholly to their Lord. As for the soul at peace ( al-nafs al-muṭmaʾinna ), there is no conflict with it: its submission has become real, and it must be left alone and loved. Having treated the safeguarding of religion, the commentary next turns to the safeguarding of human lives."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1622,"arabic_characters":1577}];
